quote = (customer: unknown, items: unknown[]): number => {\n  const [item] = items as Item[];\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + basePremiumFor(item?.type ?? \"\");\n};\n```\n\nHmm, but this adds an abstraction. Is it worth it? With more item types coming (staff, potion, runes, moonstones), having a lookup will make the code clearer.\n\nActually a simpler approach with a lookup object:\n```typescript\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n};\n\nexport const quote = (customer: unknown, items: unknown[]): number => {\n  const [item] = items as Item[];\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + (item?.type ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0 : 0);\n};\n```\n\nThis reduces conditionals and makes adding new types easier. But does it reveal intent? Yes - BASE_PREMIUMS clearly maps item types to base premiums.\n\nHowever, the existing tests expect that for empty list, return just processing fee. For unknown type, return processing fee. The lookup handles this with `?? 0`.\n\nWait, the current code does `const [item] = items as Item[];` which means for empty array, item is undefined, and returns PROCESSING_FEE. For unknown type, returns PROCESSING_FEE. Good.\n\nLet me think about another issue: the type assertion `as Item[]` is a bit unsafe. We could improve types.\n\nActually, the function signature takes `items: unknown[]`. We could use type narrowing:\n```typescript\nconst isItem = (x: unknown): x is Item => typeof x === \"object\" && x !== null && \"type\" in x && typeof (x as Item).type === \"string\";\n```\n\nBut that adds complexity. Maybe overkill for now.\n\nLet me think about APP mass after refactoring with lookup:\n\nConstants: 5 (5, 100, 60, \"sword\", \"amulet\") + 0? Wait the lookup has keys \"sword\", \"amulet\" and values 100, 60, plus PROCESSING_FEE=5. So constants = 5. Actually keys are string literals, so they count too. So 5 constants.\nBindings: customer, items, item, itemType? Wait no itemType. If we use lookup: customer, items, item = 3 b