sider. The lookup itself is a good refactor because it removes duplication and reveals intent. But should I add the explaining variable? It makes it clearer what we're adding. But `PROCESSING_FEE + (BASE_PREMIUMS[item?.type ?? \"\"] ?? 0)` is fairly clear too.\n\nLet me think about the conceptual duplication. Currently:\n- `if (item?.type === \"sword\") return PROCESSING_FEE + SWORD_BASE_PREMIUM;`\n- `if (item?.type === \"amulet\") return PROCESSING_FEE + AMULET_BASE_PREMIUM;`\n\nThe duplication is: checking item type, returning PROCESSING_FEE + base_premium. We can extract the base premium lookup, which is the part that varies.\n\nRefactored:\n```typescript\ntype Item = { type: string };\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n};\n\nexport const quote = (customer: unknown, items: unknown[]): number => {\n  const [item] = items as Item[];\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + (BASE_PREMIUMS[item?.type ?? \"\"] ?? 0);\n};\n```\n\nThis removes the two if statements and replaces with a lookup. I think this is clearer because it shows \"the base premium depends only on item type\" and makes adding new types trivial.\n\nNow calculate APP mass:\nConstants: 5 (5, 100, 60, \"sword\", \"amulet\") = 5\nBindings: customer, items, item = 3\nInvocations: 0 (property access is not invocation)\nConditionals: 2 (two `??` operators) = 8\nLoops: 0\nAssignments: 0\nTotal: 16? Wait that's same as before. Hmm.\n\nActually `??` might not count as a conditional. Let me reconsider what counts as conditional. The APP description says: \"Conditional (Mass: 4): Control flow (if, switch, ?:)\" - it explicitly mentions `?:` but not `??`. `??` is a logical/coalescing operator. Some interpretations might count it as conditional-like.\n\nIf we use ternary:\n```typescript\nreturn PROCESSING_FEE + (item?.type ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0 : 0);\n```\nThis has 1 ternary conditional = 4, plus maybe `??` = 4. So 8.\n\nHmm, the mass calculation