tem = { type: string };\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;  // constant = 1\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {  // Record<string, number> is type, not counted\n  sword: 100,  // constant 100 + constant \"sword\" = 2\n  amulet: 60,  // constant 60 + constant \"amulet\" = 2\n};\n\nexport const quote = (customer: unknown, items: unknown[]): number => {  // bindings customer, items = 2\n  const [item] = items as Item[];  // binding item = 1\n  return PROCESSING_FEE + (BASE_PREMIUMS[item?.type ?? \"\"] ?? 0);  // constants: 0, \"\" = 2; conditionals: 2 `??` = 8\n};\n```\n\nConstants: 5, 100, 60, \"sword\", \"amulet\", 0, \"\" = 7\nBindings: customer, items, item = 3\nInvocations: 0\nConditionals: 2 `??` = 8\nLoops: 0\nAssignments: 0\nTotal: 18\n\nHmm, this is higher! Because of the extra constants 0 and \"\".\n\nWhat if we avoid the empty string:\n```typescript\nreturn PROCESSING_FEE + (item?.type ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0 : 0);\n```\n\nConstants: 5, 100, 60, \"sword\", \"amulet\" = 5\nBindings: customer, items, item = 3\nInvocations: 0\nConditionals: 1 ternary + 1 `??` = 8\nLoops: 0\nAssignments: 0\nTotal: 16\n\nSame mass, but the structure is better - no duplicated if-return pattern.\n\nWhat if we avoid `??` by having a default function or using `||`:\n```typescript\nreturn PROCESSING_FEE + (item?.type ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] || 0 : 0);\n```\nSame.\n\nOr with an explaining variable:\n```typescript\nconst basePremium = item?.type ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0 : 0;\nreturn PROCESSING_FEE + basePremium;\n```\n\nConstants: 5\nBindings: customer, items, item, basePremium = 4\nInvocations: 0\nConditionals: 1 ternary + 1 `??` = 8\nTotal: 17\n\nSlightly higher but potentially clearer.\n\nActually, we can avoid the ternary by initializing BASE_PREMIUMS with a default:\n```typescript\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number | undefined> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n};\n\nconst basePremium = BASE_PREMIUMS[item?.type ?? \"\"] ?? 0;\n```\n\nNo, still has