 there are 4, no block applies, so 4 × 25 = 100.\n\nWait but \"7 runes → 175 G base premium\". 7 = 3 + 4. If block requires exactly 3, then neither 3+4 nor 7 alone qualifies. 7 × 25 = 175. So the rule is: block discount applies only when a group has exactly 3 items. Otherwise each component is full price.\n\nActually wait, \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\". So exactly 3 of same type gets block.\n\nSo rule: for each component type, if count == 3, base = 60; else base = count × 25.\n\nBut what about 6 runes? Would that be two blocks (120) or no block (150)? The spec says \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" It doesn't explicitly say multiples. But 7 runes → 175 = 7×25. So for 6 runes, if we apply two blocks: 120. If no block: 150. The spec's example of 7 runes being 175 suggests that blocks are only for exactly 3, not multiple blocks. Actually 7 runes = 175 = 7×25, no discount. 6 would be 150 = 6×25. So yes, only exactly 3 gets discount.\n\nWait but the phrasing \"A building block of 3 alike components\" suggests maybe blocks can stack? But example 7 runes contradicts that. Let me trust the examples:\n- 2 runes → 50\n- 3 runes → 60\n- 4 runes → 100\n- 7 runes → 175\n\nSo: component base premium = count × 25, but if count == 3 for a component type, replace with 60.\n\nFor 3 runes + 3 moonstones: runes count=3 → 60, moonstones count=3 → 60, total 120. ✓\n\nOK, implement that.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:54","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_08-56-23_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_kimi-k2-7/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: InsuredItem): number =>\n 