ic modifiers: curse = 50% of 100 = 50. So item premium contribution = 150. Policy base = 100. Policy-wide: first insurance = 10% of 100 = 10. Total before fee = 150 + 10 = 160. Plus fee 5 = 165.\n\nSo \"premium 160 G before fee\" refers to total before processing fee. But quote returns with fee. So test should expect 165. The test description should probably say \"premium 165 G\" or I could write the test to expect 165. Let me update the test to expect 165.\n\nActually, looking at my test list entry #12: \"should apply cursed surcharge to cursed sword -- premium 160 G before fee\". And #16: \"should handle newcomer with cursed sword -- premium 165 G\". These are basically the same scenario. Test #12 was meant to test the cursed surcharge in isolation. Since the only policy-wide modifier for a new customer is first insurance, the final is 165.\n\nI'll make test #12 expect 165 and keep description accurate. Or expect 160? Hmm.\n\nActually, to keep tests focused, maybe test #12 should use a customer with previous contracts so only item-specific applies? But the spec says each item in a quote is treated as first insurance regardless. So first insurance always applies to each quote. Unless the customer has previous contracts, then follow-up discount applies.\n\nWait, the spec: \"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\" And integration says \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\" So first insurance always applies. Then follow-up contract discount applies to contracts after the first. But how do we know if it's the first quote or follow-up? The processScenario tracks steps. For quote function in isolation, we don't know contract history. Maybe quote should receive a flag?\n\nActually the test list item #15: \"should apply first-insurance surcharge and follow-up discount on second contract\". This suggests quote needs to know contract position. Maybe we should pass an `isFirstContract` flag or contract index to quote. Let me think about this.\n\nThe scenario has steps. `processScenario` knows the step index. For quote steps, if it's the first quote step, apply first insurance surcharge; otherwise apply follow-up discount. For the `quote` function, we could pass `contractIndex` or a boolean.\n\nBut my current quote signature is `(customer, items)`. I need to add contract info. Let me reconsider the API design.\n\nActually, looking at the processScenario requirements, quote steps don't have an explicit \"is first contract\" field. The CLI determines it from step order. So the `quote` function should probably accept a parameter like `isFirstContract` or `contractIndex`.\n\nFor now, since tests are isolated, I'll design quote to take a third parameter or include it in customer. But the spec schema for customer only has yearsWithMHPCO. The operation-specific fields are in steps.\n\nHmm. For test #16, \"newcomer with cursed sword\", premium 165. First insurance applies. So quote should return 165.\n\nFor test #17, \"long-standing customer second contract\", premium 160. That includes first insurance surcharge AND follow-up discount. The follow-up discount applies because it's the second quote in the scenario.\n\nSo the `quote` function needs to know whether this is the first contract or a follow-up. Let me add a parameter `contractIndex: number` or `isFollowUp: boolean`.\n\nActually, for simplicity and to match the spec, I'll pass `isFirstContract: boolean` to quote. Or maybe a `contractNumber: number` (0-based). Let me use `contractIndex`.\n\nWait, the public API `processScenario` receives steps. The internal `quote` can take any parameters. Let me design:\n\n```ts\nexport const quote = (customer: Customer, items: InsuredItem[], contractIndex: number): number\n```\n\nFor test #12 (cursed sword, new customer, first contract): contractIndex = 0.\n- base = 100\n- curse = 50\n- policyBase = 100\n- firstInsurance = 10% of 100 = 10\n- followUp discount = 0 (first contract)\n- loyalty = 0 (0 years)\n- total = 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 fee = 165\n\nFor test #17 (long-standing customer second contract, cursed enchantment 7 sword): contractIndex = 1.\n- base = 100\n- curse = 50\n- high enchantment = 30% of 100 = 30\n- policyBase = 100\n- loyalty = 20% of 100 = 20 discount\n- firstInsurance = 10\n- followUp = 15% of 100 = 15 discount\n- total = 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 + 5 = 160\n\nOK so I need to implement modifiers. The current quote only handles base premiums.\n\nGiven this complexity, I think for test #12 I'll write it expecting 165 and include contractIndex = 0. Let me activate it.\n\nActually, test #12 description says \"premium 160 G before fee\". I think I'll change the test to expect 165 final. Or I could split it: test base premium calculation. But quote returns final. I'll just expect 165 and maybe update description.\n\nActually, I should stick to what the spec examples say. The cursed sword example in the spec: \"a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\". So for a cursed sword alone, base 100 + curse 50 + first insurance 10 = 160 before fee. Final = 165. The test description \"premium 160 G before fee\" matches the spec's \"before further modifiers and fee\" but for a single item the only policy-wide modifier is first insurance.\n\nI'll write the test to expect 165 (the actual quote output). Actually wait, the description literally says \"before fee\", so maybe I should test the pre-fee amount somehow? But the function returns with fee. Hmm.\n\nI think the cleanest is to update my test to expect the final premium 165 and note that the 160 is before fee. Let me do that.</think>"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:66","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_08-56-23_cla