urcharge.\n\nWait, first insurance surcharge is 10% of policy base premium. If policy base is 0, surcharge is 0. So empty list = 5. ✓\n\nNow update:\n- Sword: 115\n- Amulet: 71\n- Staff: 93\n- Potion: 49\n- 2 runes: 60\n- 3 runes: 71\n- 4 runes: 115\n- 7 runes: ceil(175 + 17.5 + 5) = ceil(197.5) = 198\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone: base 75 + first ins 7.5 + fee 5 = 87.5 → ceil 88\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones: base 120 + first ins 12 + fee 5 = 137\n\nWait, but the test said 80 and 125 before. Those were base + fee only. Need to update.\n\nAlso, rounding: the values that are whole numbers don't change. 115, 71, 93, 49, 60, 71, 115 are whole. 7 runes becomes 198. 2r+1m becomes 88. 3r+3m becomes 137.\n\nBut wait, the integration example says newcomer with cursed sword = 165. That's 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 = 165. Whole. ✓\n\nAnd long-standing customer second contract = 160. That's 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 + 5 = 160. ✓\n\nOK. I need to update the tests. But the workflow wants one test at a time. However, since these are pre-existing tests that need correction, I'll update them. This is not ideal but necessary.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe I should not have applied first insurance surcharge yet. The current test is just about curse. I could have implemented curse surcharge only, without first insurance. But then test #16 (newcomer with cursed sword = 165) would fail later. And the spec clearly says first insurance applies.\n\nAlternatively, I can keep first insurance and update previous tests. I think that's better.\n\nLet me update the tests. I'll use edit with multiple replacements.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I see the earlier base-premium tests now fail because the spec's first-insurance surcharge was not yet reflected in their expectations. I will correct those expectations to match the full quote rules while keeping only the current test as the active focus.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:71","name":"edit","arguments