 num) = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass: 13\n```\n\nAh, they count `+` as an invocation. So arithmetic operators are invocations.\n\nAnd `reduce` counts as a loop (iteration) AND an invocation? They counted `reduce` as invocation and also as loop. So `reduce` contributes 2 (invocation) + 5 (loop) = 7 mass.\n\nWait, in the example: Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4. Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5. So reduce is counted in both. Total for reduce = 2 + 5 = 7.\n\nOK so I need to count:\n- Function calls as invocations\n- Operators as invocations\n- Iteration methods (reduce, forEach, map) as loops AND invocations\n- for loops as loops\n\nAlso property access? The example doesn't mention it. I think property access (like `BASE_PREMIUMS[type]`) might count as invocation. But maybe not. Let me assume property access is not an invocation unless it's a method call.\n\nOK let me do a more careful mass count.\n\nCurrent code:\n\n```typescript\ntype InsuredItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material: string;\n  enchantment: number;\n  cursed: boolean;\n};\n```\nType declarations: probably not counted in APP since they have no runtime mass. Skip.\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n```\n- constant: 1 (5)\n- binding: 1 (PROCESSING_FEE)\n- assignment: 1 (const declaration is a binding+assignment)\n\nWait, is `const x = 5` counted as assignment? The example says assignments are mutations like `x = 5`, `count++`. A const declaration initializes once. I think const declarations count as binding, not assignment. The assignment mass is for reassignments/mutations. So `const x = 5` = 1 binding + 1 constant.\n\n```typescript\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\n```\n- constant: 1\n- binding: 1\n\n```typescript\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n```\n- constant: 1\n- binding: 1\n\n```typescript\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n```\n- constants: 6 (100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25)\n- binding: 1 (BASE_PREMIUMS)\n\n```typescript\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n```\n- constants: 2 (\"rune\", \"moonstone\")\n- binding: 1 (COMPONENT_TYPES)\n- invocation: 1 (new Set)\n\n```typescript\nconst componentGroupPremium = (count: number, unitPremium: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * unitPremium;\n```\n- binding: 3 (componentGroupPremium, count, unitPremium)\n- conditional: 1\n- invocation: 1 (*) - comparison `===` is also an invocation? The example counted `+`. Comparison operators might be invocations too. Let's count `===` as invocation = 1. So invocations: 2 (`===`, `*`)\n\nActually, let me count `===` as invocation since it's a binary operation. So 2 invocations.\n\n```typescript\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  const unitPremium = BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? 0;\n  return COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type) ? componentGroupPremium(count, unitPremium) : count * unitPremium;\n};\n```\n- bindings: 3 (premiumForGroup, type, count) + 1 (unitPremium) = 4\n- constants: 1 (0)\n- invocation: 1 (?? operator) + 1 (has) + 1 (componentGroupPremium) + 1 (*) + 1 (property access BASE_PREMIUMS[type]) = 5\n- conditional: 1\n\nHmm, ?? is the nullish coalescing operator. Is it an invocation? It's an operator, so yes, count as invocation.\n\nProperty access `BASE_PREMIUMS[type]` - is this an invocation? It's a computed property access. In the example, `numbers.reduce(...)` - they counted `reduce` as invocation but didn't explicitly count `numbers.reduce` property access. I think property access is not counted as invocation unless it involves a method call. But computed property access with `[]` is more like an operation. I'll count it as invocation to be conservative.\n\n```typescript\nconst countByType = (items: InsuredItem[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n```\n- bindings: 2 (countByType, items) + 1 (counts) + 1 (item) = 4\n- invocation: 1 (new Map) + 1 (set) + 1 (get) + 1 (??) + 1 (+) + 1 (property access item.type, counts.get... hmm) \n  Let me count method calls: set, get = 2. Operators: ??, + = 2. Property access: item.type (1), counts.get(item.type) - the property access to call get (1). So invocations = 4.\n- constant: 1 (0)\n- loop: 1 (for)\n- conditional: 1 (??)\n\nWait, is `??` a conditional? It's nullish coalescing, a control flow operator. The example doesn't clarify. I'll treat it as invocation (operator) not conditional. Actually, conditional mass is for `if`, `switch`, `?:`. `??` is an operator. So invocation.\n\n```typescript\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n```\nType declaration. Skip.\n\n```typescript\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  items: InsuredItem[],\n  contractIndex: number = 0,\n): number => {\n```\n- bindings: 4 (quote, customer, items, contractIndex)\n- constant: 1 (0 default)\n\n```typescript\n  let policyBasePremium = 0;\n```\n- binding: 1\n- constant: 1\n- assignment: 1 (let declaration with initialization? Or is assignment only for reassignments?)\n\nHmm, `let policyBasePremium = 0` - this is an initial assignment. The example says assignments are mutations. I think initial `let` declarations count as binding, and subsequent `=` count as assignments. But `let x = 0` is both. Let me treat the initialization as binding+constant, and `+=` as assignment. Actually, the mass calculation says \"Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations (x = 5, count++)\". `x = 5` is reassignment, not initial declaration. So `let policyBasePremium = 0` is binding + constant, not assignment. The `+=` is assignment.\n\n```typescript\n  for (const [type, count] of countByType(items)) {\n    policyBasePremium += premiumForGroup(type, count);\n  }\n```\n- loop: 1\n- bindings: 2 (type, count)\n- invocation: 1 (countByType) + 1 (premiumForGroup) + 1 (+= is assignment, not invocation) = 2\n- assignment: 1 (+=)\n\n```typescript\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n```\n- binding: 1\n- constant: 1\n\n```typescript\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (item.cursed) {\n      itemSurcharges += premiumForGroup(item.type, 1) * 0.5;\n    }\n  }\n```\n- loop: 1\n- binding: 1 (item)\n- conditional: 1 (if)\n- assignment: 1 (+=)\n- invocation: 1 (premiumForGroup) + 1 (item.type property access?) + 1 (*) = maybe 2 or 3\n- constants: 2 (1, 0.5)\n\n```typescript\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = contractIndex === 0 ? policyBasePremium * 0.1 : 0;\n```\n- binding: 1\n- conditional: 1\n- invocation: 1 (===) + 1 (*) = 2\n- constants: 2 (0, 0.1)\n\n```typescript\n  const total = PROCESSING_FEE + policyBasePremium + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge;\n```\n- binding: 1\n- invocation: 3 (+ operators)\n\n```typescript\n  return Math.ceil(total);\n```\n- invocation: 1 (Math.ceil)\n\n```typescript\nexport const claim = (policy: unknown, incident: unknown): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => ({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n```\n- bindings: 3 (claim, policy, incident)\n- constants: 2 (0, 0)\n\n```typescript\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): unknown => undefined;\n```\n- bindings: 2 (processScenario, scenario)\n- constant: 1 (undefined)\n\nNow summing up current:\n\nConstants: 1+1+1+6+2+1+2+1+1+2 = 18\nBindings: 1+1+1+1+1+3+4+4+4+1+4+1+2+1+1+1+3+2 = ... let me recount.\n\nLine by line binding count:\n1. type - skip\n2. PROCESSING_FEE: 1\n3. COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE: 1\n4. COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM: 1\n5. BASE_PREMIUMS: 1\n6. COMPONENT_TYPES: 1\n7. componentGroupPremium, count, unitPremium: 3\n8. premiumForGroup, type, count, unitPremium: 4\n9. countByType, items, counts, item: 4\n10. Customer type - skip\n11. quote, customer, items, contractIndex: 4\n12. policyBasePremium: 1\n13. type, count: 2\n14. itemSurcharges: 1\n15. item: 1\n16. firstInsuranceSurcharge: 1\n17. total: 1\n18. claim, policy, incident: 3\n19. processScenario, scenario: 2\n\nTotal bindings: 1+1+1+1+1+3+4+4+4+1+1+2+1+1+1+1+3+2 = 32\n\nInvocations:\n6. new Set: 1\n7. ===, *: 2\n8. ??, property access, has, componentGroupPremium, *: 5\n9. new Map, set, get, ??, +: 5\n11. countByType, premiumForGroup: 2\n14. premiumForGroup, *, item.type: 3\n15. ===, *: 2\n16. +, +, +: 3\n17. Math.ceil: 1\n\nTotal invocations: 1+2+5+5+2+3+2+3+1 = 24\n\nConditionals:\n7. ?:: 1\n8. ?:: 1\n9. ??: 1 (if counted as conditional) - I'll count it as invocation instead\n14. if: 1\n15. ?:: 1\n\nIf ?? is invocation: conditionals = 4\nIf ?? is conditional: conditionals = 6\nI'll use invocation for ??, so conditionals = 4.\n\nLoops:\n9. for: 1\n11. for: 1\n14. for: 1\nTotal loops: 3\n\nAssignments:\n11. +=: 1\n14. +=: 1\nTotal assignments: 2\n\nTotal current mass:\n= constants*1 + bindings*1 + invocations*2 + conditionals*4 + loops*5 + assignments*6\n= 18*1 + 32*1 + 24*2 + 4*4 + 3*5 + 2*6\n= 18 + 32 + 48 + 16 + 15 + 12\n= 141\n\nNow refactored code mass estimate:\n\n```typescript\ntype InsuredItem = { ... }; // skip\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5; // 1 const + 1 binding\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3; // 1 + 1\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60; // 1 + 1\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS = {...}; // 6 + 1\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]); // 2 + 1 + 1 invocation\n\nconst componentBlockPremium = (count: number, unitPremium: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * unitPremium;\n// bindings: 3, conditional: 1, invocations: 2 (===, *)\n\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  const unitPremium = BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? 0;\n  return COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)\n    ? componentBlockPremium(count, unitPremium)\n    : count * unitPremium;\n};\n// bindings: 4, const: 1, invocations: 5, conditional: 1\n\nconst countByType = (items: InsuredItem[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n// bindings: 4, const: 1, invocations: 5, loop: 1\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => {\n  let policyBasePremium = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countByType(items)) {\n    policyBasePremium += premiumForGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return policyBasePremium;\n};\n// bindings: 3 (basePremiumFor, items, policyBasePremium), const: 1, invocations: 2, loop: 1, assignment: 1\n\nconst cursedSurchargeFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => {\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (item.cursed) {\n      itemSurcharges += premiumForGroup(item.type, 1) * 0.5;\n    }\n  }\n  return itemSurcharges;\n};\n// bindings: 3, const: 2, invocations: 3, loop: 1, conditional: 1, assignment: 1\n\nconst firstInsuranceSurchargeFor = (\n  basePremium: number,\n  contractIndex: number,\n): number => (contractIndex === 0 ? basePremium * 0.1 : 0);\n// bindings: 3, const: 2, invocations: 2, conditional: 1\n\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number }; // skip\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  items: InsuredItem[],\n  contractIndex: number = 0,\n): number => {\n  const policyBasePremium = basePremiumFor(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = cursedSurchargeFor(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(\n    policyBasePremium,\n    contractIndex,\n  );\n  const total =\n    PROCESSING_FEE + policyBasePremium + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge;\n  return Math.ceil(total);\n};\n// bindings: 4 (quote, customer, items, contractIndex) + 4 (policyBasePremium, itemSurcharges, firstInsuranceSurcharge, total) = 8\n// const: 1 (default 0)\n// invocations: 2 (basePremiumFor, cursedSurchargeFor) + 1 (firstInsuranceSurchargeFor) + 3 (+, +, +) + 1 (Math.ceil) = 7\n\nexport const claim = (...) => ({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n// bindings: 3, const: 2\n\nexport const processScenario = (...) => undefined;\n// bindings: 2, const: 1\n```\n\nRefactored totals:\nConstants: 1+1+1+6+2+1+1+2+2+1 = 18? Wait let me recount.\n- PROCESSING_FEE: 1\n- COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE: 1\n- COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM: 1\n- BASE_PREMIUMS: 6\n- COMPONENT_TYPES: 2\n- premiumForGroup: 1 (0)\n- countByType: 1 (0)\n- basePremiumFor: 1 (0)\n- cursedSurchargeFor: 2 (0, 0.5)\n- firstInsuranceSurchargeFor: 2 (0, 0.1)\n- quote default: 1 (0)\n- claim: 2\n- processScenario: 1 (undefined)\n\nTotal constants: 1+1+1+6+2+1+1+2+2+1+2+1 = 21\n\nBindings:\n- PROCESSING_FEE: 1\n- COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE: 1\n- COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM: 1\n- BASE_PREMIUMS: 1\n- COMPONENT_TYPES: 1\n- componentBlockPremium, count, unitPremium: 3\n- premiumForGroup, type, count, unitPremium: 4\n- countByType, items, counts, item: 4\n- basePremiumFor, items, policyBasePremium: 3\n- cursedSurchargeFor, items, itemSurcharges: 3\n- firstInsuranceSurchargeFor, basePremium, contractIndex: 3\n- quote, customer, items, contractIndex, policyBasePremium, itemSurcharges, firstInsuranceSurcharge, total: 8\n- claim, policy, incident: 3\n- processScenario, scenario: 2\n\nTotal bindings: 1+1+1+1+1+3+4+4+3+3+3+8+3+2 = 38\n\nInvocations:\n- new Set: 1\n- componentBlockPremium: ===, * = 2\n- premiumForGroup: ??, property access, has, componentBlockPremium, * = 5\n- countByType: new Map, set, get, ??, + = 5\n- basePremiumFor: countByType, premiumForGroup, += (assignment) = 2\n- cursedSurchargeFor: premiumForGroup, *, item.type = 3\n- firstInsuranceSurchargeFor: ===, * = 2\n- quote: basePremiumFor, cursedSurchargeFor, firstInsuranceSurchargeFor, +, +, +, Math.ceil = 7\n\nTotal invocations: 1+2+5+5+2+3+2+7 = 27\n\nConditionals:\n- componentBlockPremium: 1\n- premiumForGroup: 1\n- cursedSurchargeFor: 1\n- firstInsuranceSurchargeFor: 1\nTotal conditionals: 4\n\nLoops:\n- countByType: 1\n- basePremiumFor: 1\n- cursedSurchargeFor: 1\nTotal loops: 3\n\nAssignments:\n- basePremiumFor: += 1\n- cursedSurchargeFor: += 1\nTotal assignments: 2\n\nRefactored total mass:\n= 21 + 38 + 27*2 + 4*4 + 3*5 + 2*6\n= 21 + 38 + 54 + 16 + 15 + 12\n= 156\n\nSo mass increases from ~141 to ~156. But Rule 2 (clarity) trumps mass.\n\nActually, my mass calculation is rough and the exact numbers might vary. The key point is that clarity improves significantly while mass increases slightly.\n\nWait, but the instructions say \"Lower mass = Better code (generally)\" and \"Rule 2 trumps APP\". So an increase in mass is acceptable if clarity improves.\n\nBut I want to make sure I'm not over-engineering. Let me consider a less invasive refactoring that still improves clarity but adds less mass.\n\nAlternative refactoring:\n\n1. Rename `componentGroupPremium` to `componentBlockPremium` (only)\n2. Extract only `basePremiumFor(items)` and `cursedSurchargeFor(items)` from `quote`\n3. Keep first insurance surcharge inline\n\nThis reduces the number of new functions by one.\n\nOr even less invasive:\n1. Rename only\n2. Keep everything in quote but add comments? No, comments don't improve APP and aren't preferred over self-documenting code.\n\nActually, I think extracting all three is fine and follows the Simple Design rules. The `quote` function becomes a clean composition of named operations.\n\nBut wait - is there duplication I'm missing? Let me check.\n\nIn `basePremiumFor` and `cursedSurchargeFor`, both use `premiumForGroup`. That's intentional reuse.\n\nIn `componentBlockPremium`, we have `count * unitPremium`. In `premiumForGroup` fallback, we also have `count * unitPremium`. This is conceptual duplication! Both calculate `count * unitPremium`.\n\nActually, that's interesting. `componentBlockPremium` handles the block discount and falls back to `count * unitPremium`. `premiumForGroup` for non-components also does `count * unitPremium`. So the expression `count * unitPremium` appears in two places.\n\nWe could refactor:\n\n```typescript\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  const unitPremium = BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? 0;\n  const standardPremium = count * unitPremium;\n  return COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE\n    ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM\n    : standardPremium;\n};\n```\n\nThis removes `componentBlockPremium` entirely and eliminates the duplicated `count * unitPremium` expression. But it inlines the block discount logic into `premiumForGroup`.\n\nMass impact:\n- Remove `componentBlockPremium` function (saves its bindings, conditional, invocations)\n- Add `standardPremium` binding\n- Add `&&` conditional operator (which is a conditional? or invocation?)\n\nHmm, `&&` is a logical operator. Is it a conditional? The APP says conditional is control flow (`if`, `switch`, `?:`). `&&` is short-circuit evaluation, a control flow operator. I'd count it as conditional or invocation. Actually, `? :` is explicitly listed. `&&` is not. I'll count `&&` as invocation (operator).\n\nBut the original `componentBlockPremium` had a ternary. The new version also has a ternary. So conditionals stay the same.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The duplication of `count * unitPremium` is real but small. Removing `componentBlockPremium` might reduce clarity because it no longer has a named function for the block discount concept. However, `premiumForGroup` already explains the concept.\n\nGiven Rule 2 trumps mass, which is clearer?\n- Version A: `componentBlockPremium(count, unitPremium)` helper\n- Version B: inline block discount in `premiumForGroup` with `standardPremium` explaining variable\n\nI think Version A is clearer because it names the block discount concept separately. But Version B reduces duplication.\n\nActually, the duplication is minimal (one multiplication expression). Per Rule 2, clarity wins. Keep the helper.\n\nOK, I'll go with extracting the three helpers and renaming `componentGroupPremium` to `componentBlockPremium`.\n\nWait, I should also consider whether I can simplify `countByType` using `reduce` or `Map.groupBy`. `Map.groupBy` is modern JavaScript. But it might not be available in the test environment. Using it would reduce lines but maybe increase mass.\n\nActually, `countByType` is fine as is. A `reduce` version:\n\n```typescript\nconst countByType = (items: InsuredItem[]): Map<string, number> =>\n  items.reduce((counts, item) => {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    return counts;\n  }, new Map<string, number>());\n```\n\nThis is more functional but the APP mass would be similar or higher due to reduce callback. And the for loop is clearer.\n\nOK, I'll proceed with the helper extraction.\n\nLet me write the refactored file and run tests.\n\nActually, one more consideration: the `customer` parameter is unused. Should I mention this in the documentation? Yes, I should note that `customer` is currently unused but retained because future tests will use it (loyalty discount). I won't remove it.\n\nLet me also double-check: will extracting helpers change the behavior? No, it's pure refactoring.\n\nLet me implement it.\n\nOne more thought: `componentGroupPremium` → `componentBlockPremium` - is this a good rename? \"Block\" is more specific because the constants use \"BLOCK\". \"Group\" is generic. Yes, good rename.\n\nLet me implement and run tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.write:3","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","content":"type InsuredItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material: string;\n  enchantment: number;\n  cursed: boolean;\n};\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n\nconst componentBlockPremium = (count: number, unitPremium: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * unitPremium;\n\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  const unitPremium = BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? 0;\n  return COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)\n    ? componentBlockPremium(count, unitPremium)\n    : count * unitPremium;\n};\n\nconst countByType = (items: InsuredItem[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => {\n  let policyBasePremium = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countByType(items)) {\n    policyBasePremium += premiumForGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return policyBasePremium;\n};\n\nconst cursedSurchargeFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => {\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (item.cursed) {\n      itemSurcharges += premiumForGroup(item.type, 1) * 0.5;\n    }\n  }\n  return itemSurcharges;\n};\n\nconst firstInsuranceSurchargeFor = (\n  basePremium: number,\n  contractIndex: number,\n): number => (contractIndex === 0 ? basePremium * 0.1 : 0);\n\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  items: InsuredItem[],\n  contractIndex: number = 0,\n): number => {\n  const policyBasePremium = basePremiumFor(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = cursedSurchargeFor(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(\n    policyBasePremium,\n    contractIndex,\n  );\n  const total =\n    PROCESSING_FEE + policyBasePremium + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge;\n  return Math.ceil(total);\n};\n\nexport const claim = (\n  policy: unknown,\n  incident: unknown,\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => ({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): unknown => undefined;\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-c