e task is asking us to make the code structure reflect the domain concepts. Both interpretations do that. Option A is simpler.\n\nLet me also think about naming. The current name `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` reveals intent reasonably well. Maybe it could be `halveDamageForHighEnchantment`. But `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` is fine.\n\n`reimbursableDamageFor` is clear.\n\n`cappedPayout` is clear.\n\n`claim` is clear.\n\nOK let me proceed with Option A. I'll:\n1. Extract `isHighEnchantment` predicate\n2. Add `isDragonMaterial` predicate\n3. Add `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` (identity or explicitly returns amount)\n4. Add `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` selector\n5. Update `reimbursableDamageFor` to use the selector\n\nWait, should I also rename `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause`? It currently takes `item` and `amount`. If I extract `isHighEnchantment`, the function can just take `amount`. That simplifies it.\n\nLet me write the refactored code and run tests.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider the identity dragon material clause. Maybe instead of identity, I should make it return `amount` with a comment explaining it's currently identity but represents the clause. Or I could just not have a separate function and inline the identity in the selector. But the task says \"explicitly model\".\n\nHmm, maybe I can model it as a no-op but named function. That makes the domain concept explicit even if the implementation is trivial.\n\nLet me also check if there are other refactoring opportunities.\n\nLooking at `quote`:\n- Lots of constants and rates\n- Functions are mostly clear\n- Could maybe extract a `discountsFor` or `surchargesFor` but not necessary\n\n`createPolicy`:\n- Clear\n\n`claim`:\n- Could extract `requestedPayoutFor` function\n- The mutation `policy.remainingCap = newRemainingCap` is a side effect; could be clearer\n\nBut the task focuses on dragon material clause and priority. I'll focus on that.\n\nLet me run tests first to make sure they're gree