t. This makes the clauses non-trivial and the priority explicit.\n\nWait, but then the deductible is inside the clauses. Let me structure it cleanly:\n\n```\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\nconst isHighEnchantment = (item: InsuredItem | undefined): boolean =>\n  !!item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD;\n\nconst isDragonMaterial = (item: InsuredItem | undefined): boolean =>\n  !!item && item.material === \"dragon\";\n\nconst reimbursementAfterDeductible = (amount: number): number =>\n  amount - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE;\n\nconst applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause = (amount: number): number =>\n  reimbursementAfterDeductible(amount * 0.5);\n\nconst applyDragonMaterialDamageClause = (amount: number): number =>\n  reimbursementAfterDeductible(amount);\n\nconst applyDamageClauseWithPriority = (\n  amount: number,\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n): number => {\n  if (isHighEnchantment(item)) return applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount);\n  if (isDragonMaterial(item)) return applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount);\n  return reimbursementAfterDeductible(amount);\n};\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n  amount: number,\n): number => {\n  if (amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Damage amount must be non-negative\");\n  return Math.max(0, applyDamageClauseWithPriority(amount, item));\n};\n```\n\nThis is explicit and non-trivial. Dragon material clause applies deductible. High enchantment applies halve then deductible. Default applies deductible. Priority is explicit.\n\nBut it duplicates `reimbursementAfterDeductible` calls. We can refactor:\n\n```\nconst applyDamageClauseWithPriority = (\n  amount: number,\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n): number => {\n  const amountAfterClause = isHighEnchantment(item) ? amount * 0.5 : amount;\n  return reimbursementAfterDeductible(amountAfterClause);\n};\n```\n\nBut then dragon material clause isn't used! The priority between dragon and default is moot because they're the same.\n\nHmm.\n\nIf I want to keep dragon material clause explicit and non-trivial, I need it to differ from default. But it can't differ without breaking tests.\n\nUnless... I redefine the \"default\" to NOT apply deductible, and dragon material clause applies deductible. Then:\n- Dragon items: amount - 100\n- Non-dragon items: amount (no deductible)? But test 1 regular sword: 500 -> 400, so non-dragon has deductible.\n\nSo default and dragon must be the same.\n\nConclusion: In the current test set, dragon material clause and default clause are identical (both apply standard deductible). The only way to model dragon material explicitly without changing behavior is to either:\n1. Make it identity before deductible (representing \"no special pre-deductible modifier\")\n2. Make it apply deductible (same as default, duplicating logic)\n\nI think option 1 is cleaner because it doesn't duplicate deductible logic. The deductible is universal. Clauses are pre-deductible modifiers. Dragon material clause currently has no pre-deductible modifier.\n\nSo my final refactor plan:\n\n```\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\nconst isHighEnchantment = (item: InsuredItem | undefined): boolean =>\n  !!item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD;\n\nconst isDragonMaterial = (item: InsuredItem | undefined): boolean =>\n  !!item && item.material === \"dragon\";\n\nconst applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause = (amount: number): number =>\n  amount * 0.5;\n\nconst applyDragonMaterialDamageClause = (amount: number): number =>\n  amount;\n\nconst applyDamageClausesWithPriority = (\n  amount: number,\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n): number => {\n  if (isHighEnchantment(item)) return applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount);\n  if (isDragonMaterial(item)) return applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount);\n  return amount;\n};\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n  amount: number,\n): number => {\n  if (amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Damage amount must be non-negative\");\n  const amountAfterClauses = applyDamageClausesWithPriority(amount, item);\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n```\n\nWait, I just realized: with this refactor, for dragon material items, `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` returns `amount` unchanged, then `reimbursableDamageFor` subtracts deductible. So the dragon material \"clause\" is essentially \"standard deductible applies\". This is explicit in the selector: we check dragon material and route to a function that returns the amount (so only universal deductible applies).\n\nBut is this really \"modeling the dragon-material reimbursement clause\"? The clause function is identity, which feels like a no-op. However, the selector explicitly acknowledges the clause's existence and its priority relative to high enchantment.\n\nMaybe I can improve naming to make it clearer:\n- `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` -> maybe `dragonMaterialDamageClause` or `reimburseDragonMaterialDamage`\n- Since it's identity, maybe name it `unmodifiedDamageForDragonMaterialClause`? That's awkward.\n\nActually, the clause might be that dragon material gets no special reduction. So `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` returning `amount` is correct: it represents the clause that dragon material items are reimbursed at face value (before deductible).\n\nOK I'll go with this.\n\nNow, should I also refactor other parts? The task focuses on dragon material and priority. I could also improve `claim` to use a helper for requested payout. But that's not required. Let me focus on the requested area.\n\nActually, let me also consider whether the name `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` still fits. Now that it only takes amount, maybe `halveDamage` or `highEnchantmentDamageReduction`. But `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` is still accurate. I'll keep it or maybe shorten to `applyHighEnchantmentClause`.\n\nHmm, naming evaluation: \"Does this name clearly describe what the function actually does based on all tests?\" \n- `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount)` - applies the high enchantment clause to an amount. Yes.\n- `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount)` - applies the dragon material clause. But it returns amount unchanged. The name is accurate in that it represents the clause, even if the clause is identity.\n- `applyDamageClausesWithPriority(amount, item)` - applies damage clauses with priority. Yes.\n- `isHighEnchantment(item)` - checks if item has high enchantment. Yes.\n- `isDragonMaterial(item)` - checks if item is dragon material. Yes.\n\nOK good.\n\nLet me also think about whether I should extract the \"requested payout\" calculation in `claim`. Currently:\n```\nlet requestedPayout = 0;\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  requestedPayout += reimbursableDamageFor(item, damage.amount);\n}\n```\n\nThis could be extracted to `requestedPayoutFor(policy, incident)`. That would improve Rule 2. But it's not in the requested scope. I'll consider it but probably skip to keep focused.\n\nActually, the task says \"Refactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Consider explicitly modeling...\" It doesn't say ONLY do that. But I should be careful not to over-refactor.\n\nLet me also check if there's duplicated logic in `quote`. The surcharge/discount functions are simple and non-duplicated. The `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` are similar but distinct concepts. Not worth changing.\n\nOK, let me implement the refactor and run tests.\n\nWait, one more thought. Maybe I should make the priority rule even more explicit by naming the priority levels or using a more descriptive function. But I think `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` is clear enough.\n\nLet me also check: should `isHighEnchantment` and `isDragonMaterial` be used elsewhere? Currently only in claim. Maybe in quote there's a similar concept? No, quote uses enchantment threshold for surcharge, not damage. Different thresholds.\n\nOK, let me implement.\n\nActually, I realize I should also consider whether the refactor should change the structure to use a \"clause registry\" or array of clauses. That would be more extensible but overkill. Rule 4 says fewest elements. Keep it simple.\n\nLet me also calculate mass for the ternary-in-reimbursable version as a comparison:\n\n```\nconst isHighEnchantment = (item) => !!item && item.enchantment >= 8;\nconst applyDragonMaterialDamageClause = (amount) => amount;\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (item, amount) => {\n  if (amount < 0) throw new Error(...);\n  const amountAfterClauses =\n    isHighEnchantment(item) ? amount * 0.5 :\n    item && item.material === \"dragon\" ? applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount) :\n    amount;\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - 100);\n};\n```\n\nThis has fewer functions. But `isDragonMaterial` is not extracted. The dragon material clause is inline partly.\n\nI think extracting `isDragonMaterial` and having a selector function is cleaner. I'll accept the mass increase.\n\nOK, final plan. I'll edit the file to:\n1. Keep DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE and HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD constants\n2. Add isHighEnchantment and isDragonMaterial predicates\n3. Simplify applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause to take only amount\n4. Add applyDragonMaterialDamageClause (identity)\n5. Add applyDamageClausesWithPriority\n6. Update reimbursableDamageFor to use the selector\n\nLet me write the edit.\n\nActually, one concern: the current `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` checks `item` for undefined. With `isHighEnchantment(item)`, the predicate handles undefined. Good.\n\nLet me also consider: in the current code, if item is undefined, `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` returns amount unchanged (because `item && ...` is false). This handles the case where damage is for an item not in policy. Tests for \"reject claim for item not in policy\" a