 6 + 14 + 12 = 44\n\nSo before = 26, after = 44. Mass increase of 18.\n\nHmm, that's a lot. But again, Rule 2 (clarity) is priority. The question is whether the clarity gain is worth it.\n\nI think the refactor significantly improves intent by:\n1. Making the dragon material clause explicit as a named concept\n2. Making the priority rule explicit in `applyDamageClausesWithPriority`\n3. Extracting predicates (`isHighEnchantment`, `isDragonMaterial`) that reveal domain language\n\nThis is a valuable trade-off.\n\nBut let me see if I can reduce mass without losing clarity. One idea: don't extract `isHighEnchantment` and `isDragonMaterial` as separate functions. Instead, inline the checks but keep named clause functions.\n\n```\nconst applyDamageClausesWithPriority = (\n  amount: number,\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n): number => {\n  if (item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD) {\n    return applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount);\n  }\n  if (item && item.material === DRAGON_MATERIAL) {\n    return applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount);\n  }\n  return amount;\n};\n```\n\nBindings saved: item (isHighEnchantment), item (isDragonMaterial) = 2\nInvocations saved: 0 (no helper invocations)\nConditionals: same (if, if, if) - but && could be counted as conditional? Not per formula.\n\nMass: bindings = 10, constants = 6, invocations = 7, conditionals = 3. Mass = 10 + 6 + 14 + 12 = 42. Slightly lower.\n\nBut we lose the named predicates. Is that worth 2 mass? Probably not.\n\nAnother idea: remove `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` and inline identity. Then we don't have a named clause function.\n\nMass: bindings -1 (amount in applyDragonMaterial), invocations -1 = 3 less. Mass = 41.\n\nBut we lose explicit clause function.\n\nI think keeping all the named functions is best for Rule 2. Mass increase is acceptable.\n\nActually, wait. I just realized that `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` being identity is a bit of a smell. Maybe I should model it dif