0, 8, \"dragon\", 0.5, 0, \"Damage...\" = 6\nInvocations: isHighEnchantment, isDragonMaterial, applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause, applyDragonMaterialDamageClause, applyDamageClausesWithPriority, reimbursementAfterDeductible (x3 inside clause functions), Math.max, Error = 9? Wait, `reimbursementAfterDeductible` is invoked inside `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause`, `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause`, and `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` (default case). So 3 invocations of reimbursementAfterDeductible. Plus the others = 3 + 5 = 8? Let me count: isHighEnchantment (1), isDragonMaterial (1), applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause (1), applyDragonMaterialDamageClause (1), applyDamageClausesWithPriority (1), reimbursementAfterDeductible (3), Math.max (1), Error (1) = 10 invocations.\n\nConditionals: if, if, if = 3\nMass = 12 + 6 + 20 + 12 = 50. Higher than current 44. Not better.\n\nAnd it duplicates deductible concept across clauses. Not ideal.\n\nSo I'll stick with my current refactor. Mass 26 -> 44. Clarity improved.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time. The key question is whether this refactor is actually an improvement. The mass increased by 18. Rule 2 says clarity over mass, but we need to be sure clarity actually improved.\n\nBefore:\n- `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount, item)` - checks item and applies clause\n- `reimbursableDamageFor` - checks negative, applies clause, subtracts deductible\n\nAfter:\n- `isHighEnchantment(item)` - predicate\n- `isDragonMaterial(item)` - predicate\n- `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount)` - applies clause\n- `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount)` - applies clause (identity)\n- `applyDamageClausesWithPriority(amount, item)` - selects clause by priority\n- `reimbursableDamageFor` - checks negative, applies clauses with priority, subtracts deductible\n\nThe after version makes the domain concepts explicit:\n- There is a high enchantment clause\n- There is a dragon material clause\n- There is a priority rule (high enchantment first, then dragon material)\n- The deductible is universal\n\nThis is a clear improvement in intent. The mass increase is the cost of making these concepts explicit.\n\nHowever, the identity dragon material clause is a bit awkward. I wonder if there's a way to make it less awkward. Maybe by naming it `dragonMaterialDamageClause` and adding a comment? Or by noting that it currently represents \"standard reimbursement before deductible\".\n\nBut adding a comment doesn't change mass. The function name already conveys the concept.\n\nActually, maybe I should rename `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` to `unreducedDragonMaterialDamage` or `standardDragonMaterialDamage` to indicate that it applies no additional reduction. But `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` is fine.\n\nOK, I'm satisfied with the refactor. Let me also consider if there are other small improvements.\n\nLooking at the full file, `processScenario` returns undefined and has a todo test. Not relevant.\n\n`claim` function:\n```\nexport const claim = (\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: { cause: string; damages: Array<{ itemType: string; amount: number }> },\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  let requestedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    requestedPayout += reimbursableDamageFor(item, damage.amount);\n  }\n  const { payout, newRemainingCap } = cappedPayout(\n    requestedPayout,\n    policy.remainingCap,\n  );\n  policy.remainingCap = newRemainingCap;\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: newRemainingCap };\n};\n```\n\nThis has a `for` loop. We could refactor to use `reduce`:\n```\nconst requestedPayout = incident.damages.reduce((total, damage) => {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  return total + reimbursableDamageFor(item, damage.amount);\n}, 0);\n```\n\nThis would reduce mass? Let's see:\nBefore: for loop (5), requestedPayout binding (1), damage binding (1), item binding (1) = 8\nAfter: requestedPayout binding (1), total binding (1), damage binding (1), reduce invocation (2) = 5\nAnd we remove the loop (5) and add an invocation (2), so net -3.\n\nBut this is outside the requested scope. The task focuses on dragon material and priority. I'll leave it.\n\nActually, the task says \"Refactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Consider explicitly modeling...\" So I can do additional refactorings if they improve quality. But I should be focused.\n\nLet me also check if `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` should maybe be named differently. `selectDamageClause`? `applyReimbursementClausesWithPriority`? The current name is clear.\n\nHmm, one more thing: the task says \"explicitly modeling the dragon-material reimbursement clause and the priority rule between clauses.\" My refactor does exactly that. Good.\n\nLet me finalize the report.\n\nWait, I should also run the tests one more time to be absolutely sure. I already did. They pass.\n\nLet me write a comprehensive report of the refactoring.\n\nI need to include:\n1. Naming evaluation\n2. Initial APP mass\n3. Refactoring applied\n4. New APP mass\n5. Mass change\n6. Tests status\n7. Rule evaluation\n\nLet me draft the report.\n\nNaming Evaluation:\n- Current name `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` is OK but mixes clause application with predicate logic.\n- New names: `isHighEnchantment`, `isDragonMaterial`, `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause`, `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause`, `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` - all clearly reveal intent.\n- Decision: Extract predicates and explicit clause functions, rename selector.\n\nInitial APP Mass (for claim damage clause code):\n- 26 (with const names as bindings)\n\nRefactoring Applied:\n- Extracted `isHighEnchantment` and `isDragonMaterial` predicates\n- Added explicit `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` (currently identity, representing no pre-deductible reduction)\n- Added `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` t