ndings. Then the count would be 24/41. But that seems wrong because `DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE` is used later as a binding.\n\nActually, I think the safest interpretation is:\n- The literal value is a constant (mass 1)\n- The name introduced by const/let/var is a binding (mass 1)\n- Function parameters are bindings (mass 1)\n\nSo `const DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100` contributes 1 binding + 1 constant.\n\nOK, mass = 26 before, 44 after.\n\nLet me write the final report.\n\nActually, one more thought: I should also mention that the dragon-material clause is currently modeled as identity because the existing test expectations indicate it applies the standard deductible (no additional pre-deductible reduction), and high-enchantment takes precedence. This is an honest assessment.\n\nAlso, I should note that `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` being identity is not dead code but an explicit model of the clause's current effect. It provides a named extension point for future rule changes.\n\nOK, report time.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Refactoring complete. All 26 tests remain green (7 todo).\n\n## Refactoring Report\n\n### Naming Evaluation (Rule 2 — first priority)\n\n- `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount, item)` mixed predicate logic with the transformation.\n- No named concept existed for the dragon-material reimbursement clause or the priority rule.\n- **Decision:** Extract domain predicates (`isHighEnchantment`, `isDragonMaterial`), give each clause its own function (`applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause`, `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause`), and introduce `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` so the priority rule is explicit in code.\n\n### Initial APP Mass (claim damage-clause code)\n\n```ts\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\nconst applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause = (\n  amount: number,\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n): number =>\n  item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD\n    ? amount * 0.5\n    : amount;\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n  amount: number,\n): number => {\n  if (amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Damage amount must be non-negative\");\n  const amountAfterClause = applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount, item);\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClause - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n```\n\n| Component    | Count | Mass |\n|--------------|-------|------|\n| Constants    | 5     | 5    |\n| Bindings     | 7     | 7    |\n| Invocations  | 3     | 6    |\n| Conditionals | 2     | 8    |\n| Loops        | 0     | 0    |\n| Assignments  | 0     | 0    |\n| **Total**    |       | **26** |\n\n### Refactoring Applied\n\n- Extracted `isHighEnchantment` and `isDragonMaterial` predicates.\n- Added explicit `applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` (currently identity — it represents that dragon-material items receive no additional pre-deductible reduction under current rules).\n- Added `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` to make the high-enchantment-over-dragon-material priority rule explicit.\n- Simplified `applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause` to be a pure amount transformation.\n- Kept the universal deductible in `reimbursableDamageFor`.\n\n### New APP Mass\n\n```ts\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst DRAGON_MATERIAL = \"dragon\";\n\nconst isHighEnchantment = (item: InsuredItem | undefined): boolean =>\n  !!item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD;\n\nconst isDragonMaterial = (item: InsuredItem | undefined): boolean =>\n  !!item && item.material === DRAGON_MATERIAL;\n\nconst applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause = (amount: number): number =>\n  amount * 0.5;\n\nconst applyDragonMaterialDamageClause = (amount: number): number => amount;\n\nconst applyDamageClausesWithPriority = (\n  amount: number,\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n): number => {\n  if (isHighEnchantment(item)) {\n    return applyHighEnchantmentDamageClause(amount);\n  }\n  if (isDragonMaterial(item)) {\n    return applyDragonMaterialDamageClause(amount);\n  }\n  return amount;\n};\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n  amount: number,\n): number => {\n  if (amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Damage amount must be non-negative\");\n  const amountAfterClauses = applyDamageClausesWithPriority(amount, item);\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n```\n\n| Component    | Count | Mass |\n|--------------|-------|------|\n| Constants    | 6     | 6    |\n| Bindings     | 12    | 12   |\n| Invocations  | 7     | 14   |\n| Conditionals | 3     | 12   |\n| Loops        | 0     | 0    |\n| Assignments  | 0     | 0    |\n| **Total**    |       | **44** |\n\n**Mass change:** 26 → 44 (Δ +18)\n\n### Simple Design Rule Evaluation\n\n1. **Tests pass** — ✓ `26 passed | 7 todo`\n2. **Reveals intent** — ✓ The dragon-material clause and the high-enchantment-over-dragon-material priority rule are now named and explicit.\n3. **No duplication** — ✓ Deductible logic remains in one place; clause logic is not duplicated.\n4. **Fewest elements** — Mass increased, but Rule 2 (clarity) takes priority over APP.\n\n### Notes\n\n`applyDragonMaterialDamageClause` is intentionally identity for the current test set: the existing expectations show dragon-material items receiving the standard deductible with no extra pre-deductible reduction. The function is not dead code — it documents the clause as a named extension point and makes the priority rule readable."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":259,"output":7591,"cacheRead":29824,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6515,"totalTokens":37674,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784970907043,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-ca40b016-85eb-4c7c-96e8-44be40465778","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"}],"stderr":"","usage":{"input":4965,"output":26809,"cacheRead":9395