W replaces nothing; the for over groups is still there! Wait, no, basePremiumFor still has `for (const [type, count] of groups)`. Did I miss that?)\n\nLet me re-read basePremiumFor after edit:\n```ts\nconst basePremiumFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => {\n  const groups = countByType(items.map((item) => item.type));\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of groups) {\n    if (!isKnownItemType(type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n    }\n    total += premiumForGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n```\n\nYes, it still has the `for (const [type, count] of groups)` loop. So basePremiumFor now has 2 loops: the map and the for. Before it had only the for.\n\nSo total loops after:\n- countByType: 1 for\n- basePremiumFor: 1 map + 1 for = 2\n- totalItemSurchargeFor: 1 reduce\n- createPolicy: 1 reduce\n- claim: 2 maps + 1 for = 3\nTotal: 1+2+1+1+3 = 8.\n\nBefore: 1+1+1+1+2 = 6.\nAfter: 8.\nSo +2 loops, not +1.\n\nMass after loops: 8 × 5 = 40.\nBefore loops: 6 × 5 = 30.\nDifference: +10.\n\nMass after total: 228 + (24-27) + (53-51) + (66-62) + (40-30) + (18-18) = 228 - 3 + 2 + 4 + 10 = 241.\n\nYes, 241. Delta +13.\n\nSo mass increases by 13. But clarity improves.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether property names in object literals should be constants. The formula says \"Literal values (5, 'hello', true)\". In `{ type: d.itemType, material: \"\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }`, the keys `type`, `material`, `enchantment`, `cursed` are identifiers, not literal values in source syntax. They are implicitly string keys but written as identifiers. I think it's reasonable to NOT count them as constants. I already excluded them. So constants removed: 3 (empty string, 0, false).\n\nIf someone counts the keys as string constants, then constants removed: 4 keys + 3 values = 7. But I'll stick with 3.\n\nOK, mass before 228, after 241, delta +13.\n\nBut Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps APP. The refactoring removes the conceptual hack of constructing fake InsuredItem objects just to satisfy the type signature of countByType. Now countByType accepts the minimal data it actually needs (type strings), making the code self-documenting and reducing coupling.\n\nActually, is there a way to reduce mass while keeping the same clarity? What if I use a generic key selector? Let me check quickly.\n\nGeneric version:\n```ts\nconst countByType = <T>(items: T[], getType: (item: T) => string): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const type = getType(item);\n    counts.set(type, (counts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n```\n\nBindings: items, getType, counts, item, type = 5 (vs current 3)\nInvocations: new Map, get, set, getType (called twice in source) = 4 (vs current 3)\nConditionals: ?? = 1 (same)\nLoops: 1 for (same)\nAssignments: const type = getType(item)? That's a binding, not assignment. counts.set is invocation. = 0 assignments? (vs current 0 since I now count set as invocation)\n\nWait, in current countByType I don't count any assignments. Good.\n\nCallers:\nbasePremiumFor: `countByType(items, (item) => item.type)` - no map, no extra binding beyond callback param item = 1 binding\nclaim: `countByType(policy.items, (item) => item.type)` and `countByType(incident.damages, (d) => d.itemType)` - no maps, 2 callback params (item, d)\n\nTotal changes from original:\ncountByType bindings: +2 (getType, type)\ncountByType invocations: +1 (getType called twice in source = 2 invocations? Wait, source has `const type = getType(item)` and then `counts.get(type)`. That's one getType invocation in source. So +1 invocation.)\ncallers: remove 3 maps (-3 invocations, -3 loops, -3 bindings: d was already there, but item is new in both callers? Wait, d existed in damage map. item is new for policy and basePremiumFor. So +2 bindings?)\n\nActually let's compare entire file:\n\nOriginal: 228.\n\nGeneric countByType:\nBindings +2 (getType,