g tests: 32\nRecent Green phase: (no recent green; claim validation tests all passed with existing implementation)\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784971211624},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"We are the refactoring subagent invoked via subagent tool. The task is to refactor src/claim-office.ts with tests in src/claim-office.spec.ts, 32 tests passing. Need run autonomously, keep tests green, must attempt at least one refactoring, evaluate naming first, apply 4 simple design rules priority, calculate APP mass before/after, document decisions.\n\nWe need read implementation and tests, run tests, then refactor. Use tools. Since this is a subagent with isolated context but we can use tools. Let's explore files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":67,"output":150,"cacheRead":6016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":112,"totalTokens":6233,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784971211646,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-49720d47-c95d-4771-9c1c-25c888e72388","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"type InsuredItem = {\n  type: string;\n  material: string;\n  enchantment: number;\n  cursed: boolean;\n};\n\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUE_MULTIPLIER = 10;\nconst CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\n\nconst isKnownItemType = (type: string): boolean => type in BASE_PREMIUMS;\n\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? 0;\n\nconst insuranceValueForType = (type: string): number =>\n  basePremiumForType(type) * INSURANCE_VALUE_MULTIPLIER;\n\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumForType(type);\n};\n\nconst countByType = (types: string[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const type of types) {\n    counts.set(type, (counts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => {\n  const groups = countByType(items.map((item) => item.type));\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of groups) {\n    if (!isKnownItemType(type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${type}`);\n    }\n    total += premiumForGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst itemSurchargeFor = (item: InsuredItem): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumForType(item.type);\n  const cursedSurcharge = item.cursed ? base * CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantmentSurcharge =\n    item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n      ? base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n      : 0;\n  return cursedSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n};\n\nconst totalItemSurchargeFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number =>\n  items.reduce((total, item) => total + itemSurchargeFor(item), 0);\n\nconst firstInsuranceSurchargeFor = (basePremium: number): number =>\n  basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n\nconst followUpDiscountFor = (\n  basePremium: number,\n  contractIndex: number,\n): number =>\n  contractIndex > 0 ? basePremium * FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n\nconst loyaltyDiscountFor = (customer: Customer, basePremium: number): number =>\n  customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD\n    ? basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE\n    : 0;\n\nexport const quote = (\n  customer: Customer,\n  items: InsuredItem[],\n  contractIndex: number = 0,\n): number => {\n  const policyBasePremium = basePremiumFor(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = totalItemSurchargeFor(items);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(policyBasePremium);\n  const followUpDiscount = followUpDiscountFor(\n    policyBasePremium,\n    contractIndex,\n  );\n  const loyaltyDiscount = loyaltyDiscountFor(customer, policyBasePremium);\n  return Math.ceil(\n    PROCESSING_FEE +\n      policyBasePremium +\n      itemSurcharges +\n      firstInsuranceSurcharge -\n      followUpDiscount -\n      loyaltyDiscount,\n  );\n};\n\nexport type Policy = {\n  items: InsuredItem[];\n  insuranceSum: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n};\n\nexport const createPolicy = (items: InsuredItem[]): Policy => {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce(\n    (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForType(item.type),\n    0,\n  );\n  return { items, insuranceSum, remainingCap: insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER };\n};\n\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_MULTIPLIER = 0.5;\n\nconst applyDamageClausesWithPriority = (\n  amount: number,\n  item?: InsuredItem,\n): number => {\n  if (item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_MULTIPLIER;\n  }\n  // Dragon-material items are reimbursed in full, so no reduction applies.\n  return amount;\n};\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (\n  item: InsuredItem | undefined,\n  amount: number,\n): number => {\n  if (amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Damage amount must be non-negative\");\n  const amountAfterClauses = applyDamageClausesWithPriority(amount, item);\n  return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n\nconst cappedPayout = (\n  requestedPayout: number,\n  remainingCap: number,\n): { payout: number; newRemainingCap: number } => {\n  const payout = Math.min(requestedPayout, remainingCap);\n  return { payout, newRemainingCap: remainingCap - payout };\n};\n\nexport const claim = (\n  policy: Policy,\n  incident: { cause: string; damages: Array<{ itemType: string; amount: number }> },\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((item) => item.type));\n  const damageCounts = countByType(\n    incident.damages.map((d) => d.itemType),\n  );\n  for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) {\n    if (!policyCounts.has(type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${type} is not part of the policy`);\n    }\n    if (count > (policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0)) {\n      throw new Error(`More damages for ${type} than policy covers`);\n    }\n  }\n  let requestedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    requestedPayout += reimbursableDamageFor(item, damage.amount);\n  }\n  const { payout, newRemainingCap } = cappedPayout(\n    requestedPayout,\n    policy.remainingCap,\n  );\n  policy.remainingCap = newRemainingCap;\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: newRemainingCap };\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): unknown => undefined;\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971213133},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { quote, claim, processScenario, createPolicy } from \"./claim-office.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"MHPCO Claim Office\", () => {\n  describe(\"quote\", () => {\n    it(\"should return premium 5 G for empty item list -- only processing fee\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      expect(quote(customer, [])).toBe(5);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote a single sword -- premium 115 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(115);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote a single amulet -- premium 71 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(71);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote a single staff -- premium 93 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"staff\", material: \"wood\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(93);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote a single potion -- premium 49 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"potion\", material: \"glass\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(49);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote 2 runes without block discount -- premium 60 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(60);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote 3 runes with block discount -- premium 71 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(71);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote 4 runes without block discount -- premium 115 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(115);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote 7 runes without block discount -- premium 198 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = Array.from({ length: 7 }, () => ({\n        type: \"rune\",\n        material: \"stone\",\n        enchantment: 0,\n        cursed: false,\n      }));\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(198);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote 2 runes + 1 moonstone as different types -- premium 88 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"moonstone\", material: \"gem\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(88);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote 3 runes + 3 moonstones as two blocks -- premium 137 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"moonstone\", material: \"gem\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"moonstone\", material: \"gem\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"moonstone\", material: \"gem\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(137);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply cursed surcharge to cursed sword -- premium 165 G final\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: true }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(165);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply high-enchantment surcharge at enchantment 5 -- premium 145 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(145);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply loyalty discount at exactly 2 years -- premium 95 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 2 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(95);\n    });\n    it(\"should apply follow-up contract discount on second contract -- premium 100 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 1)).toBe(100);\n    });\n    it(\"should handle newcomer with cursed sword -- premium 165 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: true }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(165);\n    });\n    it(\"should handle long-standing customer second contract with cursed enchanted sword -- premium 160 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 3 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 7, cursed: true }];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 1)).toBe(160);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote two swords -- premium 225 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(225);\n    });\n    it(\"should quote a mixed policy with item-specific and policy-wide modifiers -- premium 231 G\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 0, cursed: true },\n        { type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ];\n      expect(quote(customer, items, 0)).toBe(231);\n    });\n    it(\"should reject unknown item type in quote\", () => {\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 };\n      const items = [{ type: \"broomstick\", material: \"wood\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false }];\n      expect(() => quote(customer, items, 0)).toThrow(\"Unknown item type\");\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe(\"claim\", () => {\n    it(\"should pay regular sword damage 500 G minus deductible -- payout 400 G\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should pay rune damage 200 G minus deductible -- payout 100 G\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"rune\", material: \"stone\", enchantment: 0, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 200 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 100, remainingCap: 400 });\n    });\n    it(\"should apply high-enchantment clause to dragon-material sword enchantment 8 -- payout 400 G\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 8, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should apply dragon-material clause to enchantment 5 sword -- payout 700 G\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 5, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 800 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 700, remainingCap: 1300 });\n    });\n    it(\"should apply high-enchantment clause to steel sword enchantment 9 -- payout 400 G\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should apply high-enchantment clause over dragon material when both apply -- payout 400 G\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 9, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should apply deductible per damaged item in multi-item incident\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n        { type: \"amulet\", material: \"silver\", enchantment: 2, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"dragon attack\",\n        damages: [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n          { itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 300 },\n        ],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 600, remainingCap: 2600 });\n    });\n    it(\"should cap total payout at twice the insurance sum\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 2500 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident)).toEqual({ payout: 2000, remainingCap: 0 });\n    });\n    it(\"should exhaust cap across successive claims\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident1 = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident1)).toEqual({ payout: 1400, remainingCap: 600 });\n      const incident2 = {\n        cause: \"flood\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }],\n      };\n      expect(claim(policy, incident2)).toEqual({ payout: 600, remainingCap: 0 });\n    });\n    it(\"should reject claim for item not in policy\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 200 }],\n      };\n      expect(() => claim(policy, incident)).toThrow(\"not part of the policy\");\n    });\n    it(\"should reject claim with negative damage amount\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: -200 }],\n      };\n      expect(() => claim(policy, incident)).toThrow(\"non-negative\");\n    });\n    it(\"should reject claim with more damages of a type than policy covers\", () => {\n      const policy = createPolicy([\n        { type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3, cursed: false },\n      ]);\n      const incident = {\n        cause: \"fire\",\n        damages: [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 200 },\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 300 },\n        ],\n      };\n      expect(() => claim(policy, incident)).toThrow(\"More damages\");\n    });\n  });\n\n  describe(\"processScenario\", () => {\n    it.todo(\"should process quote then claim from schema example\");\n  });\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971213133},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now run tests to confirm passing.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:2","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test","timeout":120}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":5079,"output":32,"cacheRead":6208,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":8,"totalTokens":11319,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784971213134,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-21847388-4967-4c73-8f9c-b36634a4d939","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:2","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_08-56-23_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_kimi-k2-7\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_08-56-23_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_kimi-k2-7\n\n ✓ src/claim-office.spec.ts  (33 tests | 1 skipped) 6ms\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  32 passed | 1 todo (33)\n   Start at  09:20:14\n   Duration  201ms (transform 41ms, setup 1ms, collect 53ms, tests 6ms, environment 0ms, prepare 49ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971214635},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Need refactor. Must evaluate naming first. Let's inspect project config and maybe linting. Check package.json, tsconfig, maybe existing files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.ls:3","name":"ls","arguments":{"path":"."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":276,"output":45,"cacheRead":11264,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":29,"totalTokens":11585,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784971214635,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-5cb39a7a-b824-4d19-90bb-ce78fa7d6cb9","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.ls:3","toolName":"ls","content":[{"type":"text","text":".pi/\nAGENTS.md\neslint.config.mjs\nmetrics.json\nnode_modules/\npackage.json\npnpm-lock.yaml\nprompt.md\nrun.log\nsrc/\ntsconfig.json\nvitest.config.ts"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971215487},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Potential refactorings: The implementation is relatively clean. processScenario currently returns undefined. There's a todo test for processScenario. But we must keep tests green. The todo doesn't run. We can refactor to improve naming, reduce duplication, etc. But must not break tests.\n\nSimple design rules: Rule 2 naming first. Look at names:\n\n- isKnownItemType: fine.\n- basePremiumForType: fine.\n- insuranceValueForType: fine.\n- premiumForGroup: maybe 'premiumForGroup' okay; it handles component block discount. Could be 'premiumForTypeGroup'.\n- countByType: fine.\n- basePremiumFor: takes items array, computes base premium. The name 'basePremiumFor' similar to 'basePremiumForType' but for items. Might be ambiguous. Could rename to 'basePremiumForItems'.\n- itemSurchargeFor: okay; maybe 'surchargeForItem'.\n- totalItemSurchargeFor: okay.\n- firstInsuranceSurchargeFor: okay.\n- followUpDiscountFor: maybe 'followUpDiscountFor' not revealing: discount amount (positive). Could be 'followUpDiscountAmountFor'.\n- loyaltyDiscountFor: same.\n- applyDamageClausesWithPriority: returns amount after clauses, name says apply; maybe 'reducedDamageAfterPriorityClauses'.\n- reimbursableDamageFor: returns amount after deductible, okay.\n- cappedPayout: returns payout and new cap, okay.\n- claim: mutates policy.remainingCap and returns. Could be 'processClaim' but fine.\n\nNeed attempt at least one refactoring. We must calculate APP mass before/after. APP mass counting constants, bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, assignments. We need calculate for whole implementation? The instructions say calculate current code mass; for the implementation file. We can compute before and after changes. Need be transparent.\n\nLet's identify possible refactorings that reduce duplication or improve intent.\n\nPossibilities:\n\n1. Extract helper to compute discount/surcharge rates, reduce duplication in quote? The quote function calls multiple functions for surcharges/discounts and combines. Could define a `applyModifiers` helper? But minimal maybe not necessary.\n\n2. Consolidate `firstInsuranceSurchargeFor`, `followUpDiscountFor`, `loyaltyDiscountFor` are simple wrappers; but they reveal intent. However there is duplication: each calculates a rate applied to base premium with a condition. Could extract a generic `percentageOfBase` helper? But that may reduce clarity. Rule 2 clarity trumps DRY.\n\n3. `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` has a comment and only high-enchantment clause; dragon clause returns amount. Could simplify: It currently has if item exists and threshold. But the function returns amount reduced by 50% for high enchantment; dragon material no reduction. Name maybe should be `damageAfterClauseReductions` or `applyPriorityDamageClauses`. There's also a comment: \"Dragon-material items are reimbursed in full, so no reduction applies.\" This is an implicit rule. Maybe create explicit `isExemptFromDamageReduction`? But not needed for tests. Could add helper `isHighEnchantmentDamageClause`? That adds elements, not fewer.\n\n4. `countByType` duplicates logic? It uses for...of loop, assignment. Could use `Array.prototype.reduce`, but loops? APP mass: for-of loop counts as loop (5) plus assignment (6) each iteration? Counting elements: `for (const type of types)` loop 5, assignment `counts.set(...)` 6, binding `type` 1. reduce would be invocation 2 + loop 5 + assignment 6 + binding accumulator and current. Hmm.\n\n5. `claim` function mutates the passed policy object: `policy.remainingCap = newRemainingCap;` and returns newRemainingCap. This is side effect; the tests rely on it. Could refactor to create a new policy object to avoid mutation? But tests pass policy and check remainingCap across successive claims, requiring mutation. Or update policy explicitly. We could extract a helper `updateRemainingCap` to make side effect explicit? Might improve intent.\n\n6. In `claim`, there is duplication in validation and summation: `policyCounts` and `damageCounts` are maps. The check for unknown type and count > policy count. Could extract helper `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy`. That improves intent and reduces `claim` size.\n\n7. In `claim`, requestedPayout loop uses `policy.items.find` for each damage. Since damage types validated against policy counts, okay. But could group damages by type and compute. However not needed.\n\n8. `basePremiumFor` throws unknown item type. But `countByType` returns counts of types in items. Then it iterates groups. If unknown type, throw. Since tests rely on exact message \"Unknown item type: broomstick\". Fine.\n\n9. `processScenario` returns undefined. Could remove? But tests import it and expect export. No tests run for it except todo. Removing could break compilation? It is exported, imported. TypeScript may complain if import unused? No. But removing could break future tests. Better not remove.\n\n10. Use constants for magic numbers? Many constants already extracted. Could extract `ENCHANTMENT_`? Already.\n\n11. `COMPONENT_TYPES` set could be named `BLOCK_DISCOUNT_TYPES` to reveal intent. Naming evaluation: `COMPONENT_TYPES` doesn't clearly indicate it's the types eligible for block discount. Rename to `BLOCK_DISCOUNTABLE_TYPES` or `COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES` maybe better.\n\n12. `premiumForGroup`: the block discount logic: if component type and count === block size then return block premium else count * base. Could rename to `premiumForItemGroup`.\n\n13. `quote` calculates firstInsuranceSurcharge always, even for contractIndex > 0? The spec maybe first insurance surcharge applies to first contract only? Wait, `firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(policyBasePremium)` returns base * 0.1 always. In quote, it's always added regardless of contractIndex. But followUpDiscount subtracts base*0.15 for contractIndex>0. Net effect for second contract: +10% -15% = -5% discount. Tests confirm second contract quote: sword base 100 + 10 surcharge - 15 discount + fee 5 = 100 (ceil). So `firstInsuranceSurchargeFor` always returns surcharge. Name okay. Could inline? No.\n\n14. `followUpDiscountFor` returns positive discount amount; then quote subtracts it. That's clear.\n\n15. `basePremiumFor` is a function that throws if unknown type. Could we rename to `basePremiumForItems` to differentiate from `basePremiumForType`. Good.\n\n16. `createPolicy`: `remainingCap: insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER`. Could extract helper but clear.\n\n17. `cappedPayout` returns object; maybe name `applyCapToPayout`? It returns both payout and new cap. `cappedPayout` is okay.\n\n18. `claim` maybe rename to `processClaim`? But `claim` matches tests import and tests call `claim`. Cannot rename exported `claim` without breaking tests. We could rename internal function if exported alias, but not needed.\n\nWe need at least one refactoring, keep tests green. Since all tests pass, we can do a few simple refactorings. Need compute APP mass. We need decide a concrete change.\n\nPotential best improvement: Extract validation in `claim` to a named function `validateDamages`, and rename `basePremiumFor` to `basePremiumForItems`. That improves intent and may reduce mass? But adding a function increases elements. We need calculate mass before/after. The instructions: Lower mass generally better, but clarity can override. We need show if mass reduced or increased. If increased, explain clarity.\n\nAPP mass: We need count constants, bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, assignments across the implementation file. Let's define precisely. We need include all code in src/claim-office.ts (excluding type aliases? Type definitions maybe not counted? The APP mass guidelines list components but not type annotations. Likely count runtime code elements only. But exports? Function declarations? Are function declarations bindings? Possibly. We need a consistent count.\n\nLet's compute initial mass. First, list code:\n\ntype InsuredItem = { type: string; material: string; enchantment: number; cursed: boolean; };\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { sword:100, amulet:60, staff:80, potion:40, rune:25, moonstone:25 }; -> object literal keys? Count constants for literal values? 6 values? Each string value? They are literal strings and numbers. But maybe not count type annotation. We need decide. APP mass constants: literal values. The object has 12 literals (6 keys + 6 numbers). But maybe property names are not values? I'd count numeric literals 6, string literals keys maybe constants? Could ignore object structure? Hmm.\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]); -> new Set invocation, array literal with 2 strings.\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUE_MULTIPLIER = 10;\nconst CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\n\nconst isKnownItemType = (type: string): boolean => type in BASE_PREMIUMS;\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? 0;\nconst insuranceValueForType = (type: string): number => basePremiumForType(type) * INSURANCE_VALUE_MULTIPLIER;\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => { if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) { return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM; } return count * basePremiumForType(type); };\nconst countByType = (types: string[]): Map<string, number> => { const counts = new Map...; for (const type of types) { counts.set(type, (counts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1); } return counts; };\nconst basePremiumFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => { const groups = countByType(items.map((item) => item.type)); let total = 0; for (const [type, count] of groups) { if (!isKnownItemType(type)) { throw new Error(...); } total += premiumForGroup(type, count); } return total; };\nconst itemSurchargeFor = (item: InsuredItem): number => { const base = basePremiumForType(item.type); const cursedSurcharge = item.cursed ? base * CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0; const enchantmentSurcharge = item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0; return cursedSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge; };\nconst totalItemSurchargeFor = (items: InsuredItem[]): number => items.reduce((total, item) => total + itemSurchargeFor(item), 0);\nconst firstInsuranceSurchargeFor = (basePremium: number): number => basePremium * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\nconst followUpDiscountFor = (basePremium: number, contractIndex: number): number => contractIndex > 0 ? basePremium * FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\nconst loyaltyDiscountFor = (customer: Customer, basePremium: number): number => customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n\nexport const quote = (...) => { const policyBasePremium = basePremiumFor(items); const itemSurcharges = totalItemSurchargeFor(items); const firstInsuranceSurcharge = firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(policyBasePremium); const followUpDiscount = followUpDiscountFor(policyBasePremium, contractIndex); const loyaltyDiscount = loyaltyDiscountFor(customer, policyBasePremium); return Math.ceil(PROCESSING_FEE + policyBasePremium + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - followUpDiscount - loyaltyDiscount); };\n\nexport type Policy = {...};\nexport const createPolicy = (items: InsuredItem[]): Policy => { const insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForType(item.type), 0); return { items, insuranceSum, remainingCap: insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER }; };\n\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_MULTIPLIER = 0.5;\n\nconst applyDamageClausesWithPriority = (amount: number, item?: InsuredItem): number => { if (item && item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_THRESHOLD) { return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_DAMAGE_MULTIPLIER; } return amount; };\n\nconst reimbursableDamageFor = (item: InsuredItem | undefined, amount: number): number => { if (amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Damage amount must be non-negative\"); const amountAfterClauses = applyDamageClausesWithPriority(amount, item); return Math.max(0, amountAfterClauses - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE); };\n\nconst cappedPayout = (requestedPayout: number, remainingCap: number): { payout: number; newRemainingCap: number } => { const payout = Math.min(requestedPayout, remainingCap); return { payout, newRemainingCap: remainingCap - payout }; };\n\nexport const claim = (...) => { const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((item) => item.type)); const damageCounts = countByType(incident.damages.map((d) => d.itemType)); for (const [type, count] of damageCounts) { if (!policyCounts.has(type)) { throw new Error(`Item type ${type} is not part of the policy`); } if (count > (policyCounts.get(type) ?? 0)) { throw new Error(`More damages for ${type} than policy covers`); } } let requestedPayout = 0; for (const damage of incident.damages) { const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType); requestedPayout += reimbursableDamageFor(item, damage.amount); } const { payout, newRemainingCap } = cappedPayout(requestedPayout, policy.remainingCap); policy.remainingCap = newRemainingCap; return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: newRemainingCap }; };\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): unknown => undefined;\n\nWe need count mass. Since exact mass count may be subjective, but we can produce a reasonable detailed breakdown. Need before and after changes. If we rename and extract validation, we can count.\n\nNeed choose refactorings. Let's plan a set of small improvements:\n\n- Rename `COMPONENT_TYPES` to `BLOCK_DISCOUNT_TYPES` (intent).\n- Rename `basePremiumFor` to `basePremiumForItems`.\n- Extract damage validation logic from `claim` into `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy(policyCounts, damageCounts)`. This clarifies `claim` and reduces duplication of error message template? Not much duplication but clarifies.\n- Maybe rename `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` to `damageAfterPriorityClauses` to reflect it returns amount, not mutates. But function name currently verb \"apply\" okay but could imply mutation. A noun/result name is better. We can rename to `reducedDamageForPriorityClause`.\n- Rename `cappedPayout` to `payoutAfterCap`? The function returns both payout and remaining cap. `applyPayoutCap` maybe. Since it does apply cap, name okay.\n\nBut must keep tests green. Renaming internal constants/functions is fine.\n\nShould we worry about exported types `InsuredItem`, `Customer`, `Policy`? They are used in tests via import? Tests import `quote, claim, processScenario, createPolicy` only. But they use type from values? They don't import types. So renaming exports not needed.\n\nNeed run tests after changes.\n\nBut before that, calculate initial mass. Let's compute programmatically? Could use a script? APP mass counting is manual. But maybe we can approximate. Let's do a careful manual count. The final output should include component count.\n\nHowever, the instructions emphasize documenting decisions. If mass goes up due to extracting function, but clarity improves, we can justify.\n\nPotential issue: The subagent must return after completion; we don't have a tool to return to parent; just final response. We should produce a concise report with markers? The subagent is refactor agent; the parent expects summary. We should output a report: Refactoring decisions, APP mass, tests status. The parent might parse text? In the project instructions, the main agent reads summary after subagent returns. It doesn't require markers in subagent output. But our own system prompt requires mandatory markers? Those are for the main TDD agent output, not subagent. As subagent, we can output a normal report. But to be safe, we can produce structured report with all info.\n\nLet's now think about possible refactors and mass changes. We need attempt at least one. We can implement a few.\n\nLet's identify the most impactful: The `claim` function mixes validation and computation. Extract `validateDamageCounts` improves Rule 2. Also rename `basePremiumFor` to `basePremiumForItems`.\n\nWe also can rename `COMPONENT_TYPES` to `BLOCK_DISCOUNT_TYPES`. And rename `applyDamageClausesWithPriority` to `damageAfterPriorityClauses`.\n\nMaybe also rename `cappedPayout` to `payoutWithCap`? But not needed.\n\nLet's think about DRY: `quote` and `claim` both compute `countByType(items.map((item) => item.type))`. We could add a helper `countByTypeOfItems(items)`? But that introduces abstraction. It would be used in basePremiumFor (rename) and claim. Hmm `basePremiumFor` already takes items and maps; `claim` also maps. Extracting `countByTypeOfItems(items)` removes duplicated map pattern. That might be nice.\n\nPotential helper: `extractTypes(items: InsuredItem[]): string[]` or `typesFromItems`. Then `countByType(extractTypes(items))`. But adds function. It improves clarity and reduces duplication of mapping. APP mass: The duplicated mapping appears in basePremiumFor and claim. A helper would add a binding and an invocation each time but remove arrow and property access duplication. Let's examine.\n\nCurrently:\n`basePremiumFor`: `const groups = countByType(items.map((item) => item.type));` bindings: groups, items (param), item arrow param? map callback. invocations: countByType, items.map, property access? We count invocations only for function calls. Property access not counted. Conditionals: 0. Loop: 0? Actually for-of loop. Assignments: total assignments.\n\n`claim`: `const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((item) => item.type)); const damageCounts = countByType(incident.damages.map((d) => d.itemType));`\n\nThere is duplication of `countByType(...map(...))` pattern. A helper `countTypes(items)` could be used in both. But `basePremiumFor` maps InsuredItem to type; `claim` maps damages to itemType (different property). We could create `countItemTypes(items: InsuredItem[])` and `countDamageTypes(damages: Array<{itemType:string}>)`. But two helpers maybe not reduce much.\n\nMaybe better helper `typesOfItems(items)` returns array of item.type. Then in claim, use `typesOfItems(policy.items)` and `incident.damages.map((d)=>d.itemType)` separately. Not huge.\n\nMaybe leave as is.\n\nAnother duplicati