t. There's no special dragon-material handling. The test passes coincidentally because amount 800 - 100 = 700.\n\nHmm, but tests are green, so I shouldn't change behavior. The tests don't actually test dragon material no-deductible behavior.\n\nActually, let me re-read the comment: \"Dragon-material items are reimbursed in full, so no reduction applies.\" This comment is explaining that the absence of dragon-material handling is intentional because dragon material has no reduction. But wait, the code doesn't implement \"reimbursed in full\" either - it still applies deductible. The comment is misleading because the function name says \"damageAfterPriorityClauses\" but only one clause exists.\n\nThis is a naming/intent issue. The function `damageAfterPriorityClauses` doesn't reveal intent well because:\n1. It doesn't actually handle dragon material priority\n2. The comment tries to explain something that isn't in code\n\nHowever, I should be careful not to change behavior. Since tests don't cover dragon material no-deductible behavior, adding it might break hidden tests. Actually wait - adding dragon material full reimbursement would change the output of existing tests potentially. Let me think...\n\nActually, the test \"should apply dragon-material clause to enchantment 5 sword -- payout 700 G\" uses amount 800. If I add dragon material = no deductible, result would be 800, not 700. So tests would fail. So I cannot add dragon material logic without breaking tests.\n\nBut I can improve naming/intent of the existing function. The function currently only applies the high enchantment reduction. A better name might be `applyHighEnchantmentClause` or `damageAfterEnchantmentClause`. Let me consider.\n\n**APP Mass Calculation**\n\nLet me calculate the current mass.\n\nCurrent code has these functions/constants:\n- constants (literal values in const declarations): many constants = ~20\n- bindings: type declarations, parameters\n- invocations: function calls\n- conditionals: if/ternary\n- loops: for loops, reduce\n- assignments: let, const, set\n\nThis is going to be tedious. Let me focus on specific functions that can be improved.\n\nActually, let me look for duplication and other issues.\n\n**Duplication**\n- In `basePremiumForItems`, `policyCounts` and `damageCounts` use the same pattern as in `quote` (items.map(...))\n- The pattern `items.map((item) => item.type)` appears in `basePremiumForItems`, `createPolicy`, `claim`, and `processScenario`? Actually no, processScenario uses `step.items` not `items`.\n\n**Fewest Elements**\n- There are several constants that could be grouped, but that might hurt clarity.\n\n**Potential refactorings:**\n1. Rename `damageAfterPriorityClauses` to `damageAfterEnchantmentClause` or similar to better reveal intent. The current name implies it handles multiple priority clauses, but it only handles high enchantment. The comment about dragon material is confusing.\n2. Extract helper for the repeated pattern of getting types from items.\n3. In `processScenario`, the `quoteCount` could be clearer - maybe `policyCount` or `issuedPolicyCount`.\n\nLet me think about what would be a good, safe refactoring.\n\nActually, I think the biggest naming issue is `damageAfterPriorityClauses`. The function name suggests multiple clauses, but only high enchantment is implemented. The comment is a code smell. I should rename it to accurately reflect what it does.\n\nAlso, looking at `claim`: it mutates `policy.remainingCap`. The function `cappedPayout` returns `newRemainingCap` but then claim mutates the policy. This is a bit hidden. But changing this might break tests if they rely on mutation. Actually they do - the successive claims test relies on mutation. We could make this more explicit.\n\nBut the priority is naming. Let me calculate APP mass for a targeted change.\n\nLet me pick: Rename `damageAfterPriorityClauses` to `applyHighEnchantmentClause` and update the comment. Also, the comment about dragon material is misleading - I should remove or clarify it.\n\nWait, but the comment says \"priority clauses\" - in insurance terms, there might be priority clauses in the requirements. Let me check if there's a requirements file or README.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la && find . -maxdepth 2 -name '*.md' -o -name 'README*' | head -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":226,"output":1493,"cacheRead":11648,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1449,"totalTokens":13367,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784971333463,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-d2837bd8-95bf-48ac-a9de-9c4c2ae02bfc","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:3","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 98892\ndrwxr-xr-x    5 experimenter experimenter      4096 Jul 25 09:22 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 1047 experimenter experimenter    163840 Jul 25 09:22 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x    5 experimenter experimenter      4096 Jul 25 08:56 .pi\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter     11746 Jul 25 08:56 AGENTS.md\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter      1568 Jul 25 08:56 eslint.config.mjs\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter       373 Jul 25 08:56 metrics.json\ndrwxr-xr-x    9 experimenter experimenter      4096 Jul 25 08:56 node_modules\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter       537 Jul 25 08:56 package.json\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter     79090 Jul 25 08:56 pnpm-lock.yaml\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter     13225 Jul 25 08:56 prompt.md\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter 100945747 Jul 25 09:22 run.log\ndrwxr-xr-x    2 experimenter experimenter      4096 Jul 25 08:56 src\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter       236 Jul 25 08:56 tsconfig.json\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter       306 Jul 25 08:56 vitest.config.ts\n./prompt.md\n./AGENTS.md\n./.pi/AGENTS.md\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971341992},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me read the prompt to understand the requirements and what dragon material should do.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:4","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"prompt.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":499,"output":34,"cacheRead":13312,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":17,"totalTokens":13845,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784971341993,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-0ddc8901-c3dd-4976-ba08-faa46a0b16bd","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:4","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# MHPCO Claim Office Kata\n\n## Setting\n\nYou are building the policy management system for the **Most Honorable\nPrivileged Claims Office for Magical Risks and Cursed Items** (MHPCO),\nan old-fashioned insurance institution that covers magical items —\nswords, amulets, staves, potions, and components such as runes and\nmoonstones. The MHPCO is bureaucratic, slightly stingy, and proud of\nits venerable traditions.\n\n## Feature\n\nThe system supports two operations: **quote** computes the premium for\na list of items a customer wishes to insure, and **claim** processes a\ndamage report against an existing policy.\n\n## Rules\n\n### Item values and base premiums\n\nEach main item has an insurance value and a base premium according to\nthe MHPCO price list:\n\n- Sword: 1000 G insurance value, 100 G base premium\n- Amulet: 600 G / 60 G\n- Staff: 800 G / 80 G\n- Potion: 400 G / 40 G\n\nComponents — for example runes and moonstones — are insured at 250 G\neach, with a base premium of 25 G per component. A building block of\n3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\n\n### Premium modifiers\n\n- Cursed items add a 50 % risk surcharge.\n- Highly enchanted items (enchantment level ≥ 5) add a 30 % risk\n  surcharge.\n- Long-standing customers (≥ 2 years of business with MHPCO) receive a\n  20 % loyalty discount.\n- A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\n- Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\n- A 5 G processing fee is added to every premium.\n\nAll amounts are rounded to whole G in the MHPCO's favor.\n\n### Claim processing\n\nA deductible of 100 G applies per damage event. The total payout per\npolicy is capped at twice the insurance sum.\n\n- Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50 % of\n  the damage amount.\n- Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\n\n## Examples\n\nThe examples below illustrate single rules in isolation. Values shown\nare base premiums or raw payout amounts before integration with other\nmodifiers, unless stated otherwise.\n\n### Building block of 3 alike components\n\n- 2 runes → 50 G base premium\n- 3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\n- 4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\n- 7 runes → 175 G base premium\n\n### \"Alike\" components\n\n> ❓ Hmm — \"alike\" components: does that mean exactly the same type,\n> or just the same family (rune-y, gemstone-y)?\n\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block: different types)\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\n\n### Modifier scope on multi-item policies\n\n> ❓ A policy with several items: does the cursed surcharge apply to\n> the whole policy, or just to the cursed item?\n\n- a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain\n  amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed\n  surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not\n  of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\n- item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the\n  base premium of the affected item; policy-wide modifiers (loyalty,\n  first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base\n  premium (the sum of all item base premiums); the processing fee is\n  added at the very end\n\n### Modifier thresholds\n\n- customer with exactly 2 years with MHPCO → loyalty discount applies\n- sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge\n  applies; if cursed, both surcharges apply\n- sword with enchantment 4 → no high-enchantment surcharge; curse\n  surcharge applies only if cursed\n- dragon-material sword with exactly enchantment 8, damage 1000 G →\n  payout 400 G (high-enchantment clause applies, then deductible)\n\n### Deductible per damage event\n\n- a dragon attack damages an insured sword (500 G) and an insured\n  amulet (300 G); payout = 600 G (the 100 G deductible applies once\n  per damaged item)\n\n### Standard reimbursement (no special clauses)\n\n- regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 G → payout 400 G\n  (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies)\n- damage to a rune (insurance value 250 G), damage 200 G → payout\n  100 G (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; runes have no\n  enchantment level or material, so no special clause applies)\n\n### Enchantment threshold vs. dragon material\n\n- dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G\n  (both clauses apply; the 50 % rule wins, then deductible: 500 − 100)\n- dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 G → payout 700 G\n  (only the dragon-material clause applies: full reimbursement,\n  then deductible: 800 − 100)\n- steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G\n  (only the high-enchantment clause applies: 50 % first, then\n  deductible: 500 − 100)\n\n### Multiple items of the same type\n\n> ❓ What if a customer brings two of the same item — one in hand,\n> one in the backpack? Or just two swords because they fight with\n> two?\n\n- a policy covers two swords → insurance sum 2000 G (= 2×1000),\n  cap 4000 G\n- a dragon attack damages both swords; `damages` contains two\n  `{itemType: \"sword\", ...}` entries → each entry is treated as a\n  separate damage with its own deductible\n- if the `damages` array contains more entries of a given type than\n  the policy actually covers (e.g. two sword damages but only one\n  sword insured) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code; the\n  whole claim is rejected\n\n### Cap exhaustion\n\n- a policy covers a sword and an amulet → insurance sum 1600 G\n  (= 1000 + 600, the sum of the items' insurance values), cap 3200 G\n- a cursed sword (insurance value 1000 G, premium with modifiers\n  165 G) → cap 2000 G (based on the unmodified insurance value;\n  premium modifiers do not raise the cap)\n- a policy covers a sword and 3 runes (a block) → insurance sum\n  1750 G (= 1000 + 3×250); the block discount affects the premium\n  only, not the insurance sum\n- a sword is insured (insurance sum 1000 G, cap 2000 G); two successive\n  claims of 1500 G each\n- first claim → payout 1400 G, cap remaining 600 G\n- second claim → payout 600 G, cap remaining 0 G (the desired 1400 G\n  is reduced to the remaining cap)\n\n### Rounding in the MHPCO's favor\n\n- a premium calculation that yields 197.5 G → final premium 198 G\n  (rounded up)\n- a payout calculation that yields 350.5 G → final payout 350 G\n  (rounded down)\n- intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions;\n  only the final premium or payout is rounded\n\n### Edge cases\n\n- empty item list → premium 5 G (only the processing fee)\n- quote includes an item with an unknown type (e.g.\n  `{type: \"broomstick\"}`) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\n  and writes an error description to stderr; no `results` are written\n  to stdout\n- claim references a damage entry whose item is not part of the policy\n  (e.g. an amulet damaged when only a sword is insured, or an item\n  with an unknown type) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\n  and writes an error description to stderr\n- claim contains a damage entry with `amount: -200` → the CLI exits\n  with a non-zero status code and writes an error description to\n  stderr\n\n## Integration examples\n\nThe following two examples combine multiple rules to clarify how\nmodifiers stack and how customer history affects the premium.\n\n### Newcomer with a cursed sword\n\n- customer: 0 years with MHPCO, no previous contract\n- item: a cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3)\n- premium: 165 G\n- (100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G)\n\n### Long-standing customer's second contract\n\n> ❓ Wait — does \"first insurance\" mean the customer's first ever\n> contract, or the first time we see this particular item? A\n> long-standing customer with a brand-new sword: does the first\n> insurance surcharge still apply?\n\n- customer: 3 years with MHPCO; this is the customer's second `quote`\n  in the scenario\n- item: a cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7)\n- premium: 160 G\n- (100 G base + 50 G curse + 30 G high enchantment − 20 G loyalty\n  + 10 G first insurance − 15 G follow-up contract = 155 G + 5 G fee\n  = 160 G)\n- The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even\n  though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a\n  `quote` is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer\n  history.\n\n## CLI input/output format\n\nThe implementation must be exposed as a command-line executable\n`claim-office`. It reads a JSON document from stdin describing a\nscenario and writes a JSON document with the corresponding results to\nstdout.\n\nA scenario is a JSON object with a `customer` object and a `steps`\narray. The `customer` describes the single customer for the whole\nscenario. Each step has an `op` field (`\"quote\"` or `\"claim\"`) and the\noperation-specific input fields. Steps are processed sequentially;\nlater `claim` steps refer to a policy created by an earlier `quote`\nstep via its zero-based step index in a `policy` field.\n\nStdout receives a JSON object with a `results` array of the same length\nand order as the input `steps`. Each result contains:\n\n- For a `quote` step: `premium` (integer, total premium in G).\n- For a `claim` step: `payout` (integer, payout in G) and\n  `remainingCap` (integer, cap remaining on the policy after this\n  claim).\n\n### JSON Schema (normative)\n\nThe field names below are **binding**. Your implementation must use\nexactly these names; do not rename or restructure them.\n\n**Input (stdin):**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"customer\", \"steps\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"customer\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"yearsWithMHPCO\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"yearsWithMHPCO\": { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n      }\n    },\n    \"steps\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"oneOf\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Quote step\",\n            \"required\": [\"op\", \"items\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"op\": { \"const\": \"quote\" },\n              \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"array\",\n                \"items\": {\n                  \"type\": \"object\",\n                  \"required\": [\"type\"],\n                  \"properties\": {\n                    \"type\":        { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"sword | amulet | staff | potion | rune | moonstone\" },\n                    \"material\":    { \"type\": \"string\" },\n                    \"enchantment\": { \"type\": \"integer\" },\n                    \"cursed\":      { \"type\": \"boolean\" }\n                  }\n                }\n              }\n            }\n          },\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Claim step\",\n            \"required\": [\"op\", \"policy\", \"incident\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"op\":     { \"const\": \"claim\" },\n              \"policy\": { \"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"zero-based index of the quote step that created the policy\" },\n              \"incident\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\"cause\", \"damages\"],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"cause\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n                  \"damages\": {\n                    \"type\": \"array\",\n                    \"items\": {\n                      \"type\": \"object\",\n                      \"required\": [\"itemType\", \"amount\"],\n                      \"properties\": {\n                        \"itemType\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n                        \"amount\":   { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n                      }\n                    }\n                  }\n                }\n              }\n            }\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Output (stdout):**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"results\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"results\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"oneOf\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Quote result\",\n            \"required\": [\"premium\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"premium\": { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n            }\n          },\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Claim result\",\n            \"required\": [\"payout\", \"remainingCap\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"payout\":       { \"type\": \"integer\" },\n              \"remainingCap\": { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n            }\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Schema example\n\nStdin:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"customer\": {\"yearsWithMHPCO\": 5},\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\n      \"op\": \"quote\",\n      \"items\": [\n        {\"type\": \"amulet\", \"material\": \"silver\", \"enchantment\": 2, \"cursed\": false}\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"op\": \"claim\",\n      \"policy\": 0,\n      \"incident\": {\n        \"cause\": \"fire\",\n        \"damages\": [\n          {\"itemType\": \"amulet\", \"amount\": 200}\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nStdout shape:\n\n```json\n{\"results\": [{\"premium\": <integer>}, {\"payout\": <integer>, \"remainingCap\": <integer>}]}\n```\n\n## Expected Output Files\n\n- Source files implementing the logic and the `claim-office` CLI\n- Test files\n- CLI entry point at `src/cli.ts` (reads JSON from stdin, writes JSON to stdout)\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Use TypeScript for the implementation\n- The CLI must read JSON from stdin and write JSON to stdout\n- The CLI entry point must be at `src/cli.ts`\n- Internal data structures and module organization are free\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971342606},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! The prompt clearly states:\n- \"Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\"\n\nBut the current implementation does NOT implement this. The test \"should apply dragon-material clause to enchantment 5 sword -- payout 700 G\" expects 700 for amount 800, which is 800 - 100 = 700. If dragon material were fully reimbursed (no deductible), the payout would be 800, not 700. But the test expects 700.\n\nWait, let me re-read the prompt example:\n\"dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 G → payout 700 G (only the dragon-material clause applies: full reimbursement, then deductible: 800 − 100)\"\n\nHmm, \"full reimbursement\" seems to mean \"no high-enchantment reduction\" but deductible still applies. The phrase \"fully reimbursed\" in the rules is ambiguous - it could mean no percentage reduction, not no deductible.\n\nBut the current comment says \"Dragon-material items are reimbursed in full, so no reduction applies.\" This is actually correct in the sense that no reduction (percentage) applies, but the deductible still applies.\n\nOK so the code is correct. The naming of `damageAfterPriorityClauses` is still a bit off because it suggests multiple clauses. But actually there ARE multiple clauses considered: high enchantment and dragon material. The function applies the high enchantment clause, and the dragon material clause is represented by NOT applying a reduction.\n\nHmm, but the function doesn't explicitly handle dragon material. It just doesn't reduce anything for non-high-enchantment items. So the dragon material \"full reimbursement\" is implicit.\n\nActually, I think a better name might be `damageAfterSpecialClauses` or keep `damageAfterPriorityClauses` since the intent is that priority clauses are evaluated and if none apply, amount is unchanged.\n\nWait, I should also check the `claim` function. It validates `amount < 0` but doesn't validate the item type. However, `reimbursableDamageFor` takes `item: InsuredItem | undefined`, and if `item` is undefined, it just applies the deductible. But `validateDamageCounts` should catch unknown types.\n\nActually, there's a subtle issue: in `claim`, `policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType)` might return the wrong item if there are multiple items of the same type with different materials/enchantments. But since same-type items are treated identically in the current model (and tests pass), this is fine.\n\nLet me think about good refactoring candidates:\n\n1. **Naming**: `quoteCount` in `processScenario` could be clearer. It's counting how many policies/quotes have been issued. `issuedPolicyCount` or `policyIndex` might be clearer.\n\n2. **Duplication**: The pattern `items.map((item) => item.type)` appears in multiple places. We could extract `typesOf(items)` helper.\n\n3. **Clarity**: The `claim` function mutates policy.remainingCap. We could make this more explicit or extract a function.\n\n4. **Naming**: `damageAfterPriorityClauses` - I think this is acceptable but could be clearer. Maybe `applyPriorityClauses` or `amountAfterPriorityRules`.\n\nLet me look more carefully at duplication and potential extraction.\n\nActually, I see a clear duplication:\n```typescript\nconst policyCounts = countByType(policy.items.map((item) => item.type));\nconst damageCounts = countByType(incident.damages.map((d) => d.itemType));\n```\n\nAnd in `basePremiumForItems`:\n```typescript\nconst groups = countByType(items.map((item) => item.type));\n```\n\nAnd in `createPolicy`:\n```typescript\nconst insuranceSum = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForType(item.type), 0);\n```\n\nIf I extract a helper `itemTypesOf(items)` that returns `items.map(item => item.type)`, it could reduce duplication.\n\nBut wait, this might add an abstraction without much benefit. Let me check APP mass impact.\n\nLet me also check `countByType` - it counts string arrays. Maybe we could have `countItemTypes` but that would duplicate logic.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest refactor is:\n\n1. Rename `damageAfterPriorityClauses` to better reflect what it does. Given the comment, maybe `applyPriorityClauses` is better. Or `reduceByPriorityClauses`. Hmm.\n\nWait, the function currently returns amount or amount * 0.5. It's a reduction when high enchantment applies. Maybe `damageAfterReductionClauses` or `applyDamageReductions`.\n\nActually, I think the best naming improvement is to extract the high enchantment check into a named function, making the priority clauses explicit. But that adds elements.\n\nLet me think of a safe, meaningful refactor that reduces mass or improves clarity.\n\n**Option A**: Extract `itemTypesFrom(items)` helper to remove `items.map((item) => item.type)` duplication.\n\nBefore: multiple instances of `items.map((item) => item.type)`\nAfter: `itemTypesFrom(items)`\n\nThis reduces duplication (Rule 3). Let's count occurrences:\n1. `basePremiumForItems`: `items.map((item) => item.type)` - 1 invocation + 1 binding inside arrow + 1 loop\n2. `claim` policyCounts: `policy.items.map((item) => item.type)` - 1 invocation + 1 binding + 1 loop\n3. `claim` finding item: not applicable (uses find)\n\nActually `createPolicy` doesn't use map to types, it uses reduce.\n\nLet me count more precisely. The pattern `...map((item) => item.type)` appears in:\n- `basePremiumForItems`\n- `claim` (policyCounts)\n- `processScenario` doesn't use it.\n\nSo only 2 occurrences. Extracting a helper might add more mass than it saves.\n\n**Option B**: Rename `quoteCount` to `issuedPolicyCount` in `processScenario`.\n\nThis improves clarity (Rule 2) without changing behavior.\n\n**Option C**: Simplify `processScenario` quote branch.\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") {\n  const policy = createPolicy(step.items);\n  policies.push(policy);\n  const premium = quote(scenario.customer, step.items, quoteCount);\n  results.push({ premium });\n  quoteCount += 1;\n}\n```\n\nCould use `results.length` or `policies.length` instead of tracking quoteCount? No, because there could be claim steps mixed in. But quoteCount is essentially the number of policies issued so far = `policies.length`. So we could use `policies.length - 1` after push, or just use `policies.length` before push. Wait:\n\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") {\n  const policy = createPolicy(step.items);\n  policies.push(policy);\n  const premium = quote(scenario.customer, step.items, policies.length - 1);\n  results.push({ premium });\n}\n```\n\nThis eliminates `quoteCount` variable entirely. That's fewer elements (Rule 4) and removes assignment. Let me verify:\n- After push, policies.length is N (was N-1). contractIndex for this quote should be N-1 (zero-based count of previous quotes). Yes, because it's the (N-1)th quote. Correct.\n\nThis is a good refactor! It removes the `quoteCount` variable, one assignment, and makes the code clearer.\n\n**Option D**: Improve naming of `damageAfterPriorityClauses`.\n\nLet me think about this. The function applies high enchantment reduction. A clearer name might be `damageAfterSpecialClauses` or `applyDamageClauses`. But the comment explains the dragon mate